# Chiral sulfoxides: advances in asymmetric synthesis and problems with the accurate determination of the stereochemical outcome

Chiral sulfoxides are in extremely high demand in nearly every sector of the chemical industry concerned with the design and development of new synthetic reagents, drugs, and functional materials. The primary objective of this review is to update readers on the latest developments from the past five years (2011–2016) in the preparation of optically active sulfoxides. Methodologies covered include catalytic asymmetric sulfoxidation using either chemical, enzymatic, or hybrid biocatalytic means; kinetic resolution involving oxidation to sulfones, reduction to sulfides, modification of side chains, and imidation to sulfoximines; as well as various other methods including nucleophilic displacement at the sulfur atom for the desymmetrization of achiral sulfoxides, enantioselective recognition and separation based on either metal–organic frameworks (MOF’s) or host–guest chemistry, and the Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction. A second goal of this work concerns a critical discussion of the problem of the accurate determination of the stereochemical outcome of a reaction due to the self-disproportionation of enantiomers (SDE) phenomenon, particularly as it relates to chiral sulfoxides. The SDE is a little-appreciated phenomenon that can readily and spontaneously occur for scalemic samples when subjected to practically any physicochemical process. It has now been unequivocally demonstrated that ignorance in the SDE phenomenon inevitably leads to erroneous interpretation of the stereochemical outcome of catalytic enantioselective reactions, in particular, for the synthesis of chiral sulfoxides. It is hoped that this two-pronged approach to covering the chemistry of chiral sulfoxides will be appealing, engaging, and motivating for current research-active authors to respond to in their future publications in this exciting area of current research.
## 1. Introduction
Sulfur atoms that are stereogenic centers and also configurationally stable, such as those found in sulfinate esters (RSOOR)
and sulfoxides $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{R}^{1}\mathbf{R}^{2}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}\right)$ , have been known for about a century.1 The earliest examples of optically active sulfoxides2 and sulfinate esters3 were first described in 1926 and the first general method (Andersen’s methodology4) for the preparation of enantioenriched sulfoxides was first reported in 1962.5 Since then, the chemistry of chiral sulfoxides has attracted significant interest as these compounds have found numerous applications, most notably in asymmetric synthesis1,6 and as drugs.7 The wealth of synthetic methodology that has been developed for the preparation of chiral sulfoxides has been appraised in excellent reviews8 covering the literature up until 2011. In the present review the advances that have been made in the asymmetric synthesis of sulfoxides from the past five years (2011–2016) are described. The general methodologies covered include catalytic asymmetric sulfoxidation using either chemical, enzymatic, or hybrid biocatalytic means; kinetic resolution involving oxidation to sulfones, reduction to sulfides, modification of side chains, and imidation to sulfoximines; as well as various other methods including nucleophilic displacement at the sulfur atom for the desymmetrization of achiral sulfoxides, enantioselective recognition and separation based on either metal–organic frameworks (MOF’s) or host–guest chemistry, and the Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction. In contrast to previous reviews published on the chemistry of sulfoxides,8 critical discussion on a particular problem associated with the accurate determination of the stereochemical outcome of enantioselective reactions has been included. The problem stems from the fact that enantioenriched sulfoxides are prone to the phenomenon of self-disproportionation of enantiomers (SDE),9 thus potentially leading to mistakes in their evaluation of enantiopurity if due care is not taken in the workup and purification steps. The first warning of this innate property of chiral sulfoxides was reported by Prof. H. Kagan’s group in 1994.10 They showed10 that the application of routine achiral column chromatography for the work up and isolation of product sulfoxides can result in a flawed description of the stereochemical outcome of catalytic enantioselective sulfoxidation, varying, for example, from ${>}99\%$ enantiomeric excess (ee) to $73\%$ ee depending on the analyzed fraction of the eluted product.10 This warning was further conveyed by Kagan et al. in several subsequent publications11 but was generally neither heard nor acknowledged by the wider research community. Thus, out of some $700+$ papers published since 1994 dealing with catalytic asymmetric synthesis or enantiomeric determinations of chiral sulfoxides, only one review12a and four research reports $^{12b-e}$ have cited Kagan’s work. Consequently, one might postulate that many reported results concerning the asymmetric synthesis of sulfoxides may contain confusing or questionable data which cannot be rationalized by the usual steric or electronic factors. We consider that a critical discussion of anomalies and errors in the determination of the stereochemical outcome of
