## 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation
The catalyzed asymmetric sulfoxidation of unsymmetrical sulfides, e.g. methyl tolyl sulfide (1a, Scheme 1), as pioneered independently by Kagan13 and Modena,14 has been the most straightforward and generalized method for the preparation of enantioenriched chiral sulfoxides such as methyl tolyl sulfoxide (2a). And as readers will gather from the ensuing discussion, catalytic asymmetric oxidation of sulfides (Scheme 2) is still considered the most general and convenient approach for the synthesis of chiral sulfoxides with high enantiomeric excess (ee) given the regular appearance of experimental protocols which, from time-to-time, have been collected and disseminated in review articles. The results of studies conducted before 2000 are listed in a review8b from 2010 and for the period 2000–2009, studies performed during that time are comprehensively discussed in that review. Several other compilation8a,c,15–19 have also been published since 2010. A limitation of asymmetric oxidation in the synthesis of sulfoxides can be over-oxidation to the corresponding sulfones – kinetic resolution to improve the ee notwithstanding (see Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions).
## 2.1 Titanium tetra-iso-propoxide and chiral diols or tetraols
In 1984 the Katsuki and Sharpless oxidation system – based on titanium tetra-iso-propoxide $\mathrm{[Ti(O\mathrm{-}i P r)_{4}]}$ and $^{(R,R)}$ -diethyl tartrate (DET) and used originally in 1980 for the asymmetric oxidation of allylic alcohols20 – was applied for the first time to the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides as independently described by two groups simultaneously. Kagan et al. reported13 that oxidation using tert-butyl hydroperoxide (TBHP) with one equivalent of $\mathrm{Ti(O\mathrm{-iPr})_{4}},$ , two equivalents of DET, and one equivalent of water could yield a sulfoxide with an ee as high as $90\%$ while Modena et al. reported14 similar results for a system consisting of one equivalent of $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}{\cdot}\mathrm{iPr})_{4},$ a four-fold excess of DET, and also TBHP as oxidant. Since then, the procedures have been constantly modified, e.g. by the replacement of DET with new chiral dihydroxy-substituted ligands capable of forming complexes with titanium $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}{\cdot}\mathrm{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ or its tetravalent analogs and various adjustments of the reaction conditions such as solution homogeneity, oxidant, reaction temperature and so forth.
After 2010, procedures using new chiral diols instead of DET were published. Included are protocols applied to the synthesis of optically active aryl benzyl sulfoxides 4 by oxidation of the corresponding sulfides 3 with TBHP catalyzed by a complex between $\mathrm{Ti(O\mathrm{-iPr)_{4}}}$ and $^{(S,S)}$ or $^{(R,R)}$ -hydrobenzoin (5, Scheme 3 and Table 1).21
The data in Table 1 show that the process works extremely well for a range of aryl benzyl sulfides and only for some substrates were poor enantioselectivities in effect.
Moderate-to-good ee’s $(39-87\%)$ and moderate-to-good yields $(52\mathrm{-}69\%)$ were observed22 for the oxidation of alkyl aryl sulfides 1 to their corresponding sulfoxides 2 when chiral 1,3-diols 7 with a benzyl backbone were used as the chiral selector (CS) (Scheme 4). The ee’s of the products were evaluated by chiral HPLC and the absolute configurations of the predominate enantiomers – in each case the $R$ enantiomer was predominant – were determined by comparison of optical rotations with literature values. The amounts of the corresponding sulfones
6 produced in the reactions were substantial and essentially accounted for the rest of the mass.
The asymmetric oxidations of $1H$ -benzimidazolyl pyridinylmethyl sulfides 8a–d with cumene hydroperoxide (CHP) catalyzed by titanium complexes – formed in situ from $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O-iPr})_{4}$ , (S,S)-tartramides 10a–i, and water – afforded23 the pharmacologically important proton pump inhibitors esomeprazole, lansoprazole, rabeprazole, and pantoprazole (9a–d, respectively, Scheme 5) in high yields (up to $92\%$ ) and with excellent enantioselectivities (up to $96\%$ ee).
It is interesting to note that the immobilization of protected dimethyl L-tartrate on mesoporous support SBA-15 afforded24 chiral periodic mesoporous organosilicas (PMO’s) functionalized with thioester tartaric ligands 11 or 12 (Fig. 1), which for the enantioselective oxidation of methyl phenyl sulfide (1b) using CHP as oxidant yielded methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2b) with low-to-moderate enantioselectivities ( $35\mathrm{-}50\%$ ee).
The tetradentate ligand 13 (Fig. 2), designed after a detailed stereomechanistic analysis of the enantioselective $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{w})$ -catalyzed oxidation of unsymmetrical sulfides to sulfoxides, was applied to the oxidation of a series of alkyl aryl sulfides, RSAr ( ${\bf\ddot{R}}={\bf\Phi}$ methyl, ethyl; $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}\ =\ p$ -tolyl, $p$ -bromophenyl). A mixture of ligand 13, $\mathrm{Ti(O-iPr)_{4}}$ , and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ in a 1 : 1 : 1 ratio, together with 2 equivalents of CHP, provided the corresponding $(R)$ -alkyl aryl sulfoxides with higher enantioselectivities $(84-92\%$ ee) than with Kagan’s optimized methodology $(74-86\%$ ee).25
Similarly, the tetradentate, 8-quinolinol-bis-substituted BINOL ligand 14 (Fig. 3) was applied to the asymmetric oxidation of a series of 3-hydroxypropyl sulfides, ${\mathrm{RS}}(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ ( ${\bf{\bar{R}}}={\bf{\bar{\Phi}}}$ phenyl, tertbutyl, iso-propyl, benzyl, 2-pyridinyl, $n$ -hexyl). When the reaction was conducted using a mixture of ligand 14, $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O-iPr})_{4}$ $(10\mathrm{mol\%})$ , and 1.5 equivalents of $70\%$ aqueous TBHP, the corresponding (R)-3-hydroxypropyl sulfoxides were produced with enantioselectivities as high as $95\%$ ee.26 On the other hand, the oxidation of $n$ -propyl phenyl sulfide and 2-hydroxyethyl phenyl sulfide using this oxidation system proceeded with only very poor yields and enantioselectivities for the resulting sulfoxides. Hence the presence of the $\gamma$ -hydroxy group in the sulfide appears to be essential for achieving very high enantioselectivities in addition to good yields.
When Boyd et al. applied27 standard Kagan methodology – TBHP with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O-iPr})_{4}$ and DET – for the asymmetric oxidation of the cyclic 1,2-disulfides 1,2-dithiane (15) and 1,4-dihydrobenzo2,3-dithiane (17, Scheme 6) to yield thiosulfinates 16 and 18, respectively, as reference compounds as part of their study of the biotransformation of 1,2-disulfide substrates (see Section 3. Enzymatic preparation of chiral sulfoxides), only low and moderate yields of the thiosulfinates were obtained (35 and $69\%$ , respectively) and with only very poor enantioselectivities (18 and $13\%$ ee, respectively) in favor of the $R$ enantiomer. Using a chiral oxaziridine oxidant, $(-){-}(1R).$ -(10-camphorsulfonyl)- oxaziridine, provided much better yields (89 and $95\%$ , respectively) but still with only poor, though better, and fair enantioselectivities (52 and $20\%$ ee, respectively) in favor of the $s$ enantiomer. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC analysis while the absolute configuration of (S)-18 was determined by X-ray crystallographic analysis, thereby providing a means to determine the absolute configurations of the 16 enantiomers by comparison of their ECD spectra with those of the 18 enantiomers. In nonpolar solvents, 16 and 18 were found to be configurationally stable over the course of one day at ambient temperature, but in more polar solvents, e.g. water–methanol $(1:1)\/$ under the same conditions, enantiopure 16 was reduced to $17\%$ ee while enantiopure 18 was reduced to $86\%$ ee.
## 2.2 Titanium–salen(salan) catalysts
Salens are a class of bisimines derived from terminal diamines and suitably functionalized salicyl aldehydes. The simplest example, $N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(salicylidene)ethylenediamine (salen), obtained from 1,2-ethylenediamine and salicylaldehyde, lends its name to the class. Salans, on the other hand, are a class of symmetrical bisamines which can be formally derived from the corresponding salen by the reduction of the imine groups. Both classes of nitrogen-containing polyphenols are commonly used as ligands in coordination chemistry as they very easily form square-planar complexes (Fig. 4) M(salen) 19 or M(salan) 20. Chiral salen(salen)
derivatives are commonly applied in asymmetric synthesis,28 including the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides.8b
In recent years, the use of chiral titanium–salen(salan) complexes for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides has led to a substantial increase in the number of highly enantioselective protocols. Many of the complexes are based29 on $^{(R,R)-}$ or $(S,S)\ –1,2$ -diaminocyclohexane (21, Fig. 5) with the main advantage being the use of environmentally benign $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant.
One prominent method30 is based on titanium–salan complexes with $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -diphenyl substituents in the salicylidene rings of the salan ligands 22 (Fig. 6). The complexes efficiently catalyze the oxidation of aryl benzyl sulfides such as 2-naphthyl benzyl sulfide and $_{3{\bf g}}$ or alkyl aryl sulfides such as methyl 2-naphthyl sulfide and ${\bf14,b,e}$ with $30\%$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ in $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ as solvent to their corresponding sulfoxides with good-to-high ee’s if catalysts with halogen substituents at the $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -positions ( $[3,3^{\prime}\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}$ dihydrogen substituted) are applied as the CS (Table 2). However, the corresponding sulfones were always produced in substantial amounts.
Another set of titanium–salan complexes (Scheme 7),31 prepared from $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O-iPr})_{4}$ together with the salan ligands 23a–e or 24a–e and functionalized differently in the salicylidene rings, were found to efficiently catalyze the asymmetric oxidation of pyridinylmethylthiobenzimidazoles 8a,b with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to optically active (S)-omeprazole 9a and $(R)$ -lansoprazole 9b with high yields (up to ${>}95\%$ ) and enantioselectivities (up to $94\%$ ee).31 It was found that the introduction of electron-withdrawing substituents generated less active and less enantioselective catalysts and that the Ti–salen-catalyzed oxidation was non-monotonic, demonstrating isoinversion behavior with decreasing temperature.
Chiral porous metal–organic frameworks (MOF’s) 27 (Scheme 8), built from pyridinecarboxylate bridging ligands 25 and prepared by heating at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ a mixture of ${\mathrm{TiL}}(\mathbf{O}{\cdot}n\mathbf{B}\mathbf{u})_{2}$ 26 and $\mathbf{CdBr}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ in a 2 : 1 molar ratio in DMF and methanol or ethanol, were used as heterogeneous catalysts for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides with aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant. The reactions conducted in acetone at $0\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ using $30\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ (1.15 equivalents) and $1.12~\mathrm{mol\%}$ loading of $(R)–27$ afforded sulfoxides with $77-99\%$ chemoselectivities and $54\mathrm{-}90\%$ conversions with $23\substack{-62\%}$ ee’s for sulfides bearing both electron-rich and electron-poor moieties.32
A similar chiral porous zeolite-like MOF 30 (Scheme 9) was constructed33 from mixed ligands of dipyridynyl-functionalized chiral Ti(salan) 28 and biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate (BPDC, 29). The MOF, which can be easily recycled, contains salan-bound $\mathrm{Ti}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ clusters having both hydrophobic and amphiphilic mesocages. Using it as a catalyst with $30\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant for the asymmetric oxidation of a set of alkyl(aryl) aryl sulfides, $\mathbf{R(Ar)SAr}$ ( ${\bf{\dot{R}}}={\bf{\dot{\rho}}}$ methyl, iso-propyl, benzyl; $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}=p$ -X-phenyl with $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H}\mathbf{\Lambda}$ , methyl, $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}=2$ -naphthyl, $2\mathrm{-CH}_{2}$ -naphthyl, etc.), ee’s ranged widely from very poor-to-very good $5\mathrm{-}82\%$ ee). By using MOF’s as support structures however, due to a cavity confinement effect, enantioselectivities for this heterogeneous catalyst can be markedly enhanced over those for the analogous homogeneous catalyst. Thus it is an efficient as well as a recyclable heterogeneous catalyst for the enantioselective oxidation of sulfides though substantial amounts of sulfones could also be produced in the reaction $(0-25\%)$ .
Interesting helix-like $[\mathrm{TiCl}_{2}(\mathrm{salan})]$ complexes $\left(R,R\right)\mathbf{-}32\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}$ containing a (1R,2R)-diaminocyclohexane backbone and benz[a]- anthryl sidearms, prepared by the complexation of ligands 31a,b with $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ (Scheme 10), were tested34 as catalysts for the asymmetric oxidation of 1b using either CHP or $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as the oxidant. For both complexes, only modest enantioselectivities were obtained $20\mathrm{-}43\%$ ee) with complex 32a showing better enantioselectivity. However, the corresponding sulfone 6b was also always formed $\left(2\mathrm{-}15\%\right)$ .
After 2010, several protocols were published utilizing chiral Ti–salen complexes as catalysts for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides to sulfoxides with many of the complexes again based on enantiopure ${\bigl(}R,R{\bigr)}.$ - or (S,S)-1,2-diaminocyclohexane (21).29 Among them were titanium–salalen complexes 34 prepared from the phenyl-substituted salalen ligands 33 additionally functionalized in the salicylidene rings and $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}{\cdot}\mathrm{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ (Scheme 11). They were found to be efficient (TON’s, 200–300) in catalyzing the oxidation of pyridinylmethylthiobenzimidazoles 8a,b to (S)-omeprazole (9a) and $(R)$ -lansoprazole (9b) using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ with high yields (up to $96\%$ and enantioselectivities (up to $96\%$ ee).35 It was also found that this oxidation reaction was nonmonotonic, demonstrating isoinversion behavior with decreasing temperature.
The asymmetric oxidation of methyl phenyl sulfide (1b) with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ catalyzed by the reusable chiral dendritic Ti(IV)–salen catalysts 35 (Fig. 7) resulted in a chemoselectivity of $90\%$ with an ee of $85\%$ . Chiral $\operatorname{Ti}(\operatorname{Iv}).$ –salen complexes were connected to the periphery of a polyamidoamine (PAMAM) dendrimer through a flexible ionic liquid linker ( $\overset{\cdot}{\boldsymbol{n}}=1-3$ , $m=2$ , 4, or 8). The results obtained were superior to those obtained using the neat complex ( $64\%$ chemoselectivity with $68\%$ ee).36
A series of similarly reusable chiral $\operatorname{Ti}(\operatorname{Iv})$ –salen catalysts 36 (Fig. 8),37 in which two $\operatorname{Ti}(\operatorname{Iv})$ –salen units are connected with various polyethylene glycol (PEG)-based dicationic ionic liquid linkers, were also used for the asymmetric oxidation in water of three methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $[\mathrm{Ar}=$ phenyl (1b), $p$ -methoxyphenyl (1k), $o$ -methoxyphenyl], with $30\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . Sulfoxides produced with excellent enantioselectivities $85\mathrm{-}91\%$ ee) were isolated in good-to-high yields $(74-90\%)$ . The catalytic efficiency was also significantly higher than that for the neat complexes ( $10{-}16\%$ yields and $77\mathrm{-}79\%$ ee’s).
A few chiral Ti(IV)–salen catalysts 37 having thermoresponsive surfactant properties in which the $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m}).$ –salen complexes are covalently appended to the hydrophobic block of thermo-responsive amphiphilic copolymers of poly( $N\cdot$ -isopropylacrylamide-co-N,N-dimethyl acrylamide) [poly(NIPAAM$c o$ -DMAAM)] were used38 for an asymmetric oxidation in water using $30\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant (Fig. 9). Significant rate accelerations and relatively high chemo- and enantioselectivities were observed for the asymmetric oxidation of three methyl aryl sulfides, $\mathbf{MeS(O)Ar}$ $[\mathbf{Ar\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ phenyl (1b), $p$ -bromophenyl, $p$ -methoxyphenyl $\mathbf{\Pi}(\pmb{1}\mathbf{k})]$ , with almost quantitative conversions in each case $(>99\%)$ and enantioselectivities of 95, 82, and $79\%$ ee, respectively, and with the chemoselectivities of 96, 89, and $85\%$ , respectively. The neat complex by comparison was found to be far less efficient – $9\%$ conversion with $72\%$ chemoselectivity and $79\%$ ee for the oxidation of 1b to 2b. Moreover, the catalysts could be easily recovered by thermo-controlled separation and reused for several cycles.
The same authors reported a series of single-chain polymeric nanoparticles (SCPN’s) 38 (Scheme ${12})^{39}$ containing a chiral Ti(IV)–salen complex in a hydrophobic cavity availed by the amphiphilic copolymers of poly[NIPAAm- $_{\cdot c o}$ -IL/Ti(salen)] [NIPAAm, $N\mathrm{.}$ -iso-propylacrylamide; IL/Ti(salen), vinylimidazolium ionic liquid-modified chiral salen $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m})$ complex]. These SCPN’s behaved as enzyme-mimetic catalysts for the enantioselective oxidation in water of eight alkyl aryl sulfides, RSAr ( ${\bf\ddot{R}}=$ methyl, ethyl, $n$ -butyl, $n$ -hexyl; $\mathbf{Ar}=\mathbf{\partial}$ phenyl, $p$ -methoxyphenyl, $p$ -bromophenyl, $p$ -nitrophenyl, $o$ -methoxyphenyl; the set includes $^{1\mathbf{b},\mathbf{d},\mathbf{g},\mathbf{k})}$ , with $30\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , providing the respective sulfoxides with moderate-to-excellent conversions (70 to ${>}99\%$ ), high-to-excellent chemoselectivities (87 to ${>}99\%$ ), and high-toexcellent ee’s $(84-99\%)$ . These values compared well to the neat catalyst with values of $40\mathrm{-}62\%$ , $65\mathrm{-}87\%$ , and $45\mathrm{-}81\%$ ee, respectively. Ee’s were determined by chiral HPLC and the predominant enantiomer was not explicitly stated for each case except for 1b where the $R$ enantiomer was identified. The catalysts 38 could also be recovered by thermo-controlled separation.
The oxidation of sulfides to their respective sulfoxides with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was also catalyzed40 by a series of complexes 40 generated in situ from Schiff bases 39 (derived from chiral amino alcohols) with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}{\cdot}\mathrm{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ (Scheme 13). The methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $\mathbf{\hat{A}}\mathbf{r}\ =\mathbf{\nabla}p$ -X-phenyl with $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H}\mathbf{\Lambda}$ , methyl, Cl, Br, F, I), were all converted to their corresponding sulfoxides with moderate-togood conversions $(49\mathrm{-}81\%)$ , high chemoselectivities $(82\mathrm{-}97\%)$ , and poor-to-good enantioselectivities ( $5\%$ ee). Resulting ee’s were assessed as being strongly influenced by the nature of the aryl substituents.
The same group also reported a series of bimetallic titanium complexes generated in situ from $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}{\cdot}\mathrm{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ and dimeric Schiff bases 41 prepared from various chiral amino alcohols (Fig. 10).41 These complexes were used for the asymmetric oxidation of alkyl aryl sulfides, RSAr ( ${\bf\ddot{R}}=$ methyl, ethyl; $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}=p$ -X-phenyl with $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H} $ , methyl, Cl, Br, F, I, $\mathrm{NO}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ), using aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant. It was found that the use of methanol or tert-butanol as an additive improved both the conversion yield and the enantioselectivity of the reaction. By such means, the aforementioned alkyl aryl sulfides and 1,3-dithianes were oxidized to their corresponding sulfoxides with good-to-high enantioselectivities $(78-99\%$ ee) together with substrate conversions of $70–99\%$ and chemoselectivities of $89-95\%$ .
## 2.3 Catalysts based on complexes of chiral multifunctional ligands with vanadium
Vanadium is another transition metal that has found broad application as a component of chiral catalytic systems for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides to sulfoxides,56 and additionally for the kinetic resolution of sulfoxides (see Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions). However, the first vanadium catalysts based on salen complexes applied to the oxidation of alkyl aryl sulfides using an organic hydroperoxide as oxidant were not very effective.42 The corresponding sulfoxides were obtained with only low enantioselectivities, ${<}40\%$ ee, poor in comparison to the enantioselectivities obtained with titanium catalysts. A more effective vanadium catalyst was discovered by Bolm and Bienewald in 1995.43 In this case, the active catalyst was generated in situ from vanadium(IV) acetylacetonate, ${\mathrm{VO}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{2}$ , and the chiral Schiff-base ligand derived from tert-leucinol. The synthetic protocol was also more convenient as it used $30\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ (1.1 equivalents) as oxidant. Significantly, the enantioselectivity of the reaction attained a value of $85\%$ ee. Since that time, many other vanadium catalysts have been discovered and, similarly to the Bolm’s catalytic system, they are mostly based on chiral amino alcohols.
Twenty chiral Schiff-base ligands (Scheme 14) derived from 3-exo-amino-iso-borneol (42 and 43) and 3-endo-aminoborneol (44 and 45) were synthesized44 and applied in the vanadiumcatalyzed oxidation of ethyl 2-naphthyl sulfide (1l) using $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ as solvent and $30\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant (1.2 equivalents)
at room temperature. The chiral vanadium complexes were generated in situ from vanadium(IV) acetylacetonate $[\mathrm{VO}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}]$ and used in a proportion of $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ with the ligand at $1.5\mathrm{mol\%}$ . Depending on the structure of the ligands, ethyl 2-naphthyl sulfoxide (2l) was obtained with moderate-to-excellent yields $(71-94\%)$ and poor-to-excellent enantioselectivities $38\mathrm{-}90\%$ ee) under the standard conditions. Ee’s were determined by chiral HPLC with the enantiomers assigned by comparison of their optical rotations to literature. Ee’s resulting from endo-type ligands 44 and 45 were generally higher than those obtained from the exo-type ligands 42 and 43. Of note, while the former pair yielded $s$ enantioenriched product, the latter pair yielded product enriched in the $R$ enantiomer. The vanadium complex with ligand 45b was the most effective and with optimal reaction conditions – reduction of the temperature to $-10\mathrm{~\textdegreeC}$ and increasing the amount of oxidant to 1.3 equivalents – enantiopure $(99.9\%$ ee) (S)-ethyl 2-naphthyl sulfoxide (2l) was obtained in $72\%$ yield. The vanadium complex using ligand 45b was also used44 for the kinetic resolution of racemic 2l (see also Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions) since considerable amounts of the sulfone 6l were always produced and it was apparent that kinetic resolution was also contributing to the final high ee’s. The substrate scope of ligand 45b was tested on eleven other sulfides, viz. $n$ -butyl phenyl sulfide (1g), benzyl phenyl sulfide (3g), ethyl 1-naphthyl sulfide, and a set of methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $[\mathrm{Ar}=2$ -naphthyl, 1-naphthyl, phenyl (1b), $p$ -tolyl (1a), $m$ -tolyl, $p{\cdot}$ -bromophenyl, $p$ -methoxyphenyl, $p$ -nitrophenyl], with moderate-to-good yields $(60-74\%)$ and high-toexcellent enantioselectivities $85\mathrm{-}97\%$ ee’s) for the corresponding sulfoxides. The combination of ligand 45b with other transition metals, e.g. Fe(III) and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ , failed to exhibit significant enantioselectivity in the oxidation of 1l (6 and $0\%$ ee, respectively).
Chiral Schiff bases 46 and 47 (Fig. 11) were also used45 as ligands for the vanadium-catalyzed oxidation of methyl phenyl $(\mathbf{16})$ , methyl $p$ -bromophenyl, and benzyl phenyl (3g) sulfides. The catalysts based on ligands 46a–c were the more effective $(30-37\%$ ee) with enantioselectivities dependent on the structure of the sulfides.
Chiral imines 48 and 49 and amines 50 and 51 (Fig. 12) were similarly used as ligands in a vanadium-catalyzed oxidation. Ligands 48 and 49 were easily synthesized by condensation of 5-alkyl thiophene-2-carboaldehydes with the enantiomers of norephedrine. Reduction of the double bonds in 48 and 49 with $\mathrm{LiAlH_{4}}$ then afforded 50 and 51. The vanadium complexes generated46 in situ using ${\mathrm{VO}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{2}$ induced high enantioselectivities for the oxidation of methyl aryl sulfides with catalysts made from ligands 48 and 49 proving the more effective. For ligand 49, methyl aryl sulfoxides, MeS(O)Ar ( $\mathbf{Ar}=\mathbf{\partial}$ phenyl, $p$ -tolyl, $o$ -tolyl, $p$ -nitrophenyl), were obtained in yields up to $90\%$ with ee’s of $70–99\%$ .
Chiral Schiff bases 52 and 53 (Fig. 13) were also used as ligands for a similar vanadium-catalyzed oxidation of 1b providing sulfoxide 2b in similar yields $(78\%)$ but with only lowto-moderate enantioselectivities, $(9\%$ ee for 52 and $45\%$ ee for 53).47 The Schiff bases 53 and 54 were also applied to the synthesis of the biologically active sulfoxide $(R)$ -modafinil (55), an important drug used for the treatment of sleep disorders. Though 55 was obtained in high yield, only modest ee’s of ca. $35\%$ were obtained with higher enantioselectivity being exhibited by ligand 53.
Chiral ligands 56 derived from aromatic aldehydes and chiral amino alcohols were used for the preparation of oxovanadium(IV) complexes 57 and 58 (Scheme $15)^{48}$ and applied as catalysts for the oxidation of methyl phenyl sulfide (1b). The oxidation conducted with $1\mathrm{\mol\%}$ of the isolated catalyst in $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ as solvent and using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant afforded (S)-methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2b) in high yields $(>90\%)$ with high chemoselectivities (only $4{-}6\%$ of methyl phenyl sulfone was produced) and good enantioselectivities (ca. $60\%$ ee). With complexes generated in situ, similar yields and chemoselectivities but slightly lower enantioselectivities $(42-58\%$ ee) were obtained.48
The catalytic capabilities of helical vanadium–salen complexes $^{(R,R)-59}$ , $^{(R,R)}$ -60, $(R)$ -61, and $(R)$ -62 (Fig. 14), generated from appropriate ligands and ${\mathrm{VO}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{2}$ , were tested also using 1b as substrate and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant. However, although the chemoselectivities were good, only low-to-moderate enantioselectivities were obtained $(8-33\%$ ee).49
The oxidation of methyl phenyl (1b) and phenyl benzyl (3g) sulfides catalyzed by a set of nine oxido-bridged dinuclear vanadium(V) complexes 63 (Fig. 15) was conducted50 at room temperature with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant and proceeded with good yields (up to $90\%$ ) and high enantioselectivities, $71\mathrm{-}87\%$ ee for methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2b) and $61{-}69\%$ ee for phenyl benzyl sulfoxide (4g). The enantioselectivity for 2b was improved by lowering the temperature to $-20{}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ to obtain ee’s in the range $90\mathrm{-}94\%$ . The vanadium(V) complexes 63 were obtained from vanadium(V) oxytriethoxide and the corresponding Schiff bases derived from $(R)\ –(-)$ -phenylglycinol.
Hexagonal silica gel MCM-41 was used as a matrix for the preparation of two mesoporous vanadium catalysts, $\mathbf{V-MCM_{550}}$ and $\mathbf{V-MCM}_{750},$ by impregnation of the silica gel with vanadium(V) oxytri-iso-propoxide $[\mathrm{{VO}({O i P r})_{3}}].^{5}$ 1 Vanadium could be coordinated to the mesoporous walls by either tripodal, bipodal, or monopodal modes (Fig. 16); in the case of $\mathbf{V-MCM_{550}}$ , tripodal and bipodal vanadium coordination dominated while for $\mathbf{V-MCM_{750}}$ , monopodal and bipodal vanadium coordination dominated. These mesoporous vanadium materials were used as a vanadium source in the oxidation of sulfide 1b with CHP or TBHP as oxidant in the presence of $(S,S)\ –(+)\ –N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(3,5-di-tert-butylsalicylidene)-1,2-cyclohexanediamine $_{[(S,S)-64]}$ as the chiral ligand. Both oxidants afforded sulfoxide 2b with only low chemoselectivities $(11-52\%$ of sulfone 6b was produced) and very low enantioselectivities, $3\%$ ee for $\mathbf{V-MCM_{550}}$ and $11-19\%$ ee for $\mathbf{V-MCM}_{750},$ . The higher enantioselectivity achieved for $\mathbf{V-MCM_{750}}$ catalyst was related to the higher presence of monopodal and bipodal vanadium coordination modes in its structure.51
The catalytic capability of the oxo-vanadium(IV) complex derived from $(1R,2S)–(+)$ -cis-1-amino-2-indanol and immobilized on mesoporous silica SBA-15 (65, Fig. 17) for the oxidation of methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $[\mathrm{Ar}=\mathrm{Ph}$ (1b), $p$ -bromophenyl, $p$ -chlorophenyl], was compared with the neat oxo-vanadium– indanol complex. The catalytic oxidation conducted with both catalysts afforded the corresponding enantioenriched sulfoxides with comparable chemoselectivities (ca. $96\%$ ) and enantioselectivities ( $33\%$ ee). However, the mesoporous catalyst was more stable and could be reused several times without significant loss of activity.52
A convenient protocol for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides to their corresponding sulfoxides using ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ as an oxidant at room temperature employed the chiral oxo-vanadium complex 66 (Fig. 18), synthesized from $(+){}.$ -pseudoephedrine and immobilized on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles (MNP’s) as a heterogeneous and reusable catalyst. The oxidation of a series of sulfides proceeded quickly $\mathrm{<}15\ \mathrm{min})$ with good chemoselectivities (up to $97\%$ ), however, only low enantioselectivities were obtained $(9-15\%$ ee). When the oxidation was conducted at lower temperature $(-20{}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}).$ the ee’s increased up to $27\%$ . A decided advantage of this procedure was that the catalyst could be easily removed due to its magnetic properties and reused up to twenty times.53
The chiral, salen-type ligands 67 bearing spacers of different lengths (Fig. 19) and immobilized on polysiloxane were tested in a vanadium-catalyzed oxidation using benzyl phenyl sulfide $_{39}$ as substrate and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant. The catalysts were generated in situ using $\mathrm{vo}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}$ . The oxidation generally proceeded with good yields $(71-76\%)$ while the enantioselectivity depended on the length of the spacer in the ligand: catalysts were more enantioselective the longer the spacer. Thus, sulfoxide ${\bf49}$ was obtained with 35 and $38\%$ ee when using 67b and 67c, respectively, but with only $14\%$ ee when using ligand 67a.54
The results, moreover, provided selection of the best working ligand, viz. 67c, for the preparation of an active stationary phase for capillary electrophoresis55 and on-column gas chromatography.54 For this purpose, 67c was converted to a vanadium complex immobilized on polysiloxane, and then, due to the presence of the $\mathrm{{\si-H}}$ bond, used for the modification of fused-silica capillaries (Fig. 20) allowing the study of the kinetics of the oxidation process.
Duttwyler et al., while developing56 a nucleophilic arylation method for the preparation of phenolic $p$ -tolyl sulfoxides (see Section 5. Other methods) that was unsuitable for the preparation of phenolic tert-butyl sulfoxides 70, alternatively developed an asymmetric catalyzed oxidation to access the tert-butyl sulfoxides 70 (Scheme 16). The phenyl-substituted phenol 69 was reacted with di-tert-butyl disulfide in the presence of nBuLi and the resulting sulfide 71 oxidized to the phenolic tert-butyl sulfoxide 70a by $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ followed by removal of the MOM protecting group using $6\mathrm{~M~HCl}$ to yield 70b. The oxidation was catalyzed by ${\mathrm{VO}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{2}$ and the chiral Schiff-base ligand 72.
The overall yield for the three steps to 70b was $42\%$ with an ee of $91\%$ . The $R$ enantiomer was predominant as proven by X-ray crystallography.
## 2.4 Catalysts based on complexes of chiral multifunctional ligands with iron
After 2010, several protocols describing the use of complexes between iron and optically active multifunctional ligands as catalysts for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides to optically active sulfoxides have been published, including an Fe(salen)- based MOF for the kinetic resolution of sulfoxides (see Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions).
An oxidation protocol based on an iron catalyst formed in situ from $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{3}$ and $(R,R){\cdot}(-){\cdot}R,N^{\prime}$ -bis(3,5-di-tert-butylsalicylidene)-1,2-cyclohexanediamine $\left[\left(R,R\right)-64\right]$ (Fig. 21) as a chiral ligand and conducted preferentially in water using $70\%$ aqueous BTHP converted57 a series of sulfides, RSAr ( ${\bf\ddot{R}}=$ methyl, ethyl; $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}=p$ -X-phenyl with $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H}\mathbf{\Lambda}$ , methyl, Cl, Br, F, I, $\mathrm{NO}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ , to their corresponding sulfoxides in good-to-excellent yields $\left(81-99.4\%\right)$ and enantioselectivities ( $81.5\substack{-99.4\%}$ ee). Notably, over-oxidation to the sulfones did not occur.
Similarly, the iron complex 74 (Scheme 17) – formed from the amino alcohol-derived, Schiff-base ligand 73 and generated in situ by treating the ligand with ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}$ – was found to catalyze efficiently the oxidation of sulfides using aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant to provide the corresponding sulfoxides with moderate-to-excellent substrate conversions $(72-92\%)$ , excellent chemoselectivities $(93-98\%)$ , and good-to-excellent enantioselectivities $(75-96\%$ ee).58 Of note, electron-donating benzoic acid derivatives were found to improve both conversion and ee’s.
Two chiral porous Fe(salen)-based MOF’s, 76 and 77, were constructed59 (Scheme 18) using mixed ligands of dipyridinylfunctionalized chiral Fe(salen) 75 and BPDC (29). Both MOF’s were found to be useful and recyclable heterogeneous catalysts for the oxidation of selected sulfides to sulfoxides by various oxidants with iodosomesitylene (MesIO) proving superior. For example, using MOF’s 76 and 77 with $1.5\mathrm{\mol\%}$ MOF and 2 equivalents of MesIO, for phenyl iso-propyl sulfoxide (2e), ee’s reached 55 and $60\%$ as determined by chiral HPLC together with $\sim100$ and $94\%$ conversion of the phenyl iso-propyl sulfide (1e) and 89 and effectively $100\%$ chemoselectivity against the phenyl iso-propyl sulfone (6e), respectively. Using an (S,S)-configured catalyst resulted in $s$ enantioenriched products being obtained and vice versa. If the reaction time was extended however, thereby reducing the chemoselectivity to $27\%$ as a consequence of over-oxidation, the ee of the remaining sulfoxide 2e increased to $96\%$ as a result of kinetic resolution (see also Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions). Other sulfides tested included benzyl 2-naphthyl sulfide, naphthalene-2-ylmethyl 2-naphthyl sulfide, and 3,5-bis(3,5- di-tert-butyl benzyloxy) 2-naphthyl sulfide providing conversions (60, 28, and $0\%$ , respectively), chemoselectivities (98 and $95\%$ and n.a., respectively), and ee’s of (36 and $9\%$ ee and n.a., respectively) thus alluding to the steric limitations of the MOF by comparison to the free catalyst $\big[\big(\mathrm{FeL}_{2}\big(\mathrm{OAc}\big)\big)\big]$ which provided conversions ( $\sim100$ , 95, and $77\%$ , respectively), chemoselectivities (47, 81, and $94\%$ , respectively), and ee’s of (54, 21, and $11\%$ ee, respectively). The main advantages of the MOF’s over the free catalyst are improved yields (e.g. 94 vs. $78\%$ conversion with effectively $100\%$ chemoselectivity and $60\%$ ee for MOF 77) and ease of recovery of the intact and viable catalyst, while the main disadvantage is the inordinately increased reaction time (ca. three-fold longer). Otherwise the performance of the free catalyst and the MOF’s are essentially the same for unhindered substrates.
The encapsulation of Fe(salen) inside the nanocages of mesoporous silica molecular sieve SBA-16 or a new silica material called modified mesocellular foam (m-MCF), which combines large cage sizes $(15{-}22~\mathrm{nm})$ and narrow entrance sizes $\left(1.8\mathrm{-}3.7\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ , provided two solid catalysts which were applied60 for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfide 1b and benzyl phenyl sulfide $_{3{\bf g}}$ as model substrates. Chiral Fe(salen) trapped in m-MCF materials showed higher activity than the complex immobilized on SBA-16. The chemoselectivity $(93-99\%)$ , conversion $(4\small{-}94\%)$ , and enantioselectivity $(55\mathrm{-}74\%$ ee) of the encapsulated catalysts based on m-MCF were comparable with those obtained for their ‘‘free’’ Fe(salen) counterpart for the given conditions.
## 2.5 Catalysts based on complexes of chiral multifunctional ligands with manganese
It was reported61 that chiral $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{m}).$ –salen catalyst 78a (Jacobsen’s catalyst, Fig. 22) and its doubly (78b) and triply (78c) immobilized analogs dispersed finely into imidazolium ionic liquid BMImX $\left(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{Im}^{+}=1-n\right.$ -butyl-3-methylimidazolium; ${\bf X}^{-}={\bf B}{{\bf F}_{4}}^{-}$ ) behave as efficient heterogeneous catalysts for the asymmetric oxidation of methyl phenyl sulfide (1b), 2-(methylthio)pyridine, and 2-(methylthio)thiophene, with aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . The oxidation proceeded smoothly (TOF values from 12–21, conversion from $49-85\%)$ but afforded the corresponding sulfoxides with wildly variable enantioselectivities $21-92\%$ ee) with both catalysts 78b and 78c affording sulfoxides with higher ee’s in comparison to Jacobsen’s catalyst 78a. Catalyst 78c also had superior recycling behavior $60\%$ ee and $55\%$ conversion after five cycles).61
Chiral and easily accessible one-dimensional coordination polymers 79 (Fig. 23) containing chiral salen manganese metalloligands and lanthanide knots were used as catalysts for the oxidation of an extended family of alkyl aryl sulfides using iodosobenzene as oxidant. Sulfides were converted into their corresponding sulfoxides with poor-to-excellent conversions $(36\mathrm{-}100\%)$ and acceptableto-very good chemoselectivities $(76-92\%)$ , but with only very poor enantioselectivities $(3-25\%$ ee).
Easily prepared63 salan-like ligands 80 (Fig. 24) containing an additional porphyrin-inspired chiral 4,5-dihydro-1,3-oxazole substituent were tested for their asymmetric catalytic activity with $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}(\mathbf{O}\mathrm{Tf})_{2}$ using $36\%$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . Ligand 80b proved superior showing excellent tolerance for a variety of functional groups and efficiently induced very high enantioselectivities for the
$$
{\bf a})\mathrm{R}=\mathrm{Ph},{\bf b})\mathrm{R}=i\mathrm{Pr},{\bf c})\mathrm{R}=\mathrm{Bn},{\bf d})i\mathrm{Bu},{\bf e})\mathrm{R}=s\mathrm{Bu}
$$
oxidation of a large set of alkyl aryl sulfides. With optimal conditions, the alkyl aryl sulfides, RSAr ( $\mathbf{\bar{R}_{\alpha}}=\mathbf{\Phi}$ methyl, ethyl, $n$ -propyl, $n$ -pentyl, benzyl, functionalized alkyl, cyclopropyl, etc. altogether twenty-seven examples), were efficiently (yields $69\mathrm{-}92\%)$ and rapidly (0.5–1 hour) converted to their corresponding sulfoxides with excellent enantioselectivities, in most cases with 87 to ${>}99\%$ ee, using only $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ of $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{OTf})_{2}$ and 80b. The $^{\mathbf{\alpha}_{S,S}}$ enantiomer of ligand 80b preferentially produced the $R$ enantiomer except in the case of esomeprazole (9a) where the $s$ enantiomer was produced in $82\%$ yield with an ee of $90\%$ . In line with the expectation that since the oxidations of the sulfides to their corresponding sulfoxides were highly enantioselective and therefore kinetic resolution of sulfoxides by further oxidation to sulfones should be successful, kinetic resolution of sulfoxides by further oxidation to sulfones was attempted (see Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions). Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and absolute configurations determined by comparison of HPLC retention order with literature.
Later, after testing the same set of ligands 80 with a set of fifteen additives, the same group found64 that the oxidation system worked even better in the presence of catalytic amounts of adamantoic acid in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN},$ iso-propanol (1 : 2 ratio, and again finding that ligand 80b was superior though $50\%$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was used) giving the corresponding sulfoxides from a set of thirty-one compounds in good-to-excellent isolated yields $(76-95\%)$ and with very good-to-excellent enantioselectivities (80 to ${>}99\%$ ), including the pharmaceutically important esomeprazole (9a, yield, $76\%$ ; $87\%$ ee in favor of the $s$ enantiomer) and albendazole (yield, $48\%$ ; $97\%$ ee in favor of the $s$ enantiomer). Also again, based on expectations that kinetic resolution of sulfoxides by further oxidation to sulfones should be successful, kinetic resolution of sulfoxides was also attempted (see Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions). Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC. The same group also recently tested this modified oxidation system in a flow micro-reactor and reported65 that in the presence of $\mathbf{Mn(OTf)}_{2}$ and the optically active ligand 80b, a series of alkyl aryl and aryl benzyl sulfides were converted upon treatment with $45\%$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to their corresponding sulfoxides in $82\mathrm{-}91\%$ yields with $90\mathrm{-}99\%$ ee in $1{-}4\ \mathrm{min}$ . The method is thus amenable to producing gram quantities of sulfoxides efficiently in a very short amount of time.
A water-soluble, chiral manganese porphyrin complex 81 (Fig. 25) was applied as catalyst using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ for the oxidation of a series of methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $\mathbf{\DeltaAr}\ =\ p$ -X-phenyl with ${\bf X}={\bf\Phi}$ methyl, methoxy, Br, $\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ , and benzyl sulfides, BnSR ( ${\bf\ddot{R}}=$ methyl, phenyl), at $25\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The corresponding sulfoxides, including the precursor of the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug sulindac (82, Fig. 26), were prepared with up to $100\%$ conversion and up to $57\%$ ee.66 To date, sulindac (82) has only been administered as a racemate66,67 and though it is a prodrug for the achiral sulfide, the rates of enzymatic reduction to the sulfide, enzymatic oxidation to the inactive sulfone, and enzymatic re-oxidation of the sulfide back to the sulfoxide differ for the two enantiomers.67
## 2.6 Catalysts based on complexes of chiral multifunctional ligands with molybdenum
Complexes of molybdenum(VI) with the tridentate Schiff base derived from $(+)\ –(S)\cdot$ 1-amino-2-propanol with salicylaldehyde and its derivatives 83 (Fig. 27) were able to catalyze68 the oxidation of methyl phenyl sulfide (1b) and benzyl phenyl sulfide (3g) to their corresponding sulfoxides 2b and ${\bf49}$ , respectively, using $30\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ in good-to-excellent yields $(73-97\%$ ; TON’s, 73–91) but with only poor enantioselectivities $3\mathrm{-}17\%$ ee).
Similar activity was observed for the molybdenum complexes 84 and 85 generated from a series of Schiff-base ligands derived from 6-aminoglucose and various salicylaldehyde derivatives (Fig. 28) and which revealed different steric and electronic effects.69 These ligands were tested for their catalytic activity using 1b as substrate whereby it was found that catalysts formed in situ performed superior to pre-prepared complexes, e.g. conversions were 48 and $33\%$ , respectively, chemoselectivities were either $99\%$ or better, while ee’s were 10 and $3\%$ , respectively. A benzylidene protecting group at positions C-4 and C-6 of the sugar ring improved enantioselectivity, as did tert-butyl groups positioned at C-3 and C-5 of the salicylaldehyde moiety with both inclusions together providing a cumulative effect, though only poor ee’s were obtained, e.g. $0\%$ ee was obtained with neither of the inclusions (85a), $4\%$ ee with inclusion of the tertbutyl groups (85b), $8\%$ ee with inclusion of the benzylidene group (84a), and $10\%$ ee with the inclusion of both (84g), with an $R$ configuration prevalent in each of these cases. Steric effects determined not only the ee of the product, but also the absolute configuration. Moreover, sulfone formation decreased inversely with respect to the steric demand of the Schiff-base ligand.
Modest ee’s, up to $56\%$ , were obtained for the oxidation of 1b using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and catalyzed by a complex between molybdate and ligands 86 derived from $\upbeta$ -cyclodextrin (Fig. 29).70
The chiral bisguanidinium dinuclear oxodiperoxomolybdosulfate $\mathrm{\small{[BG]}^{2+}}[(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{{SO}_{4}})\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathbf{O}_{2})_{2}(\mathbf{O}_{2})_{2}]^{2-}$ ion pair 87 (Fig. 30) was applied as a catalyst for the enantioselective oxidation of a range of functionalized benzyl alkyl $\mathrm{\Gamma\big(ArCH_{2}S C H_{2}C O_{2}\it t B u\big)}$ ), dialkyl $\left(\mathrm{RSCH_{2}C O_{2}}t\mathrm{Bu}\right)$ and alkyl aryl $\left(\mathbf{ArSCH_{2}C O_{2}}t\mathbf{B}\mathbf{u}\right)$ sulfides using aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant.71 The corresponding optically active sulfoxides, altogether twenty-four examples, were isolated in high yields $(72-99\%)$ and with ee’s in the range $65\mathrm{-}99\%$ . It is worth noting that a gram-scale synthesis of $(R)$ -modafinil (55) (Fig. 13), a commercial drug, was achieved using this procedure.
Galindo et al. reported72 the molybdenum-catalyzed oxidation of sulfide 1b to sulfoxide 2b using an imidazolium-based dicarboxylic acid derived from L-valine as the CS. While chemoselectivity and substrate conversion were generally good under a variety of conditions with optimum values of 95 and $94\%$ , respectively, ee’s of only $40\%$ were obtained. However, the ee of 2b was improved by the ensuing kinetic resolution (see also Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions).
## 2.7 Catalysts based on complexes of chiral multifunctional ligands with copper
The copper(II)–salan complexes 88 (Fig. 31) were tested as oxidation catalysts using as model substrate methyl phenyl sulfide (1b) and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant.73 At room temperature they only exhibited low-to-moderate catalytic activity (conversions from $3{-}45\%$ ) and, moreover, only afforded sulfoxide 2b with very low enantioselectivities $(1-3\%$ ee). It was proposed that the saturated coordination environment around the copper atom is responsible for the low catalytic activities in comparison to the Ti– and V–salan analogs.
Much higher enantioselectivities were observed for the copper-catalyzed oxidation of aryl benzyl and alkyl aryl sulfides using aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant where the catalyst was generated by mixing copper(II) acetate with imine-type ligands 89 (Fig. 32) derived from optically active $\upbeta$ -aminoalcohols and salicyl aldehydes.74 It was found that the steric effects of the sulfide substituents strongly influenced the enantioselectivity. Under the optimized conditions, a number of alkyl aryl and aryl benzyl sulfides afforded their respective sulfoxides in excellent yields (in most cases up to $92\%$ ) with good-toexcellent enantioselectivities (up to $97\%$ ee).
Chiral copper MOF crystals 90 based on $(R)$ -BINOL (Fig. 33) were used as a heterogeneous catalyst for an asymmetric oxidation using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant.75 A series of optically active alkyl aryl sulfoxides, $\mathbf{RS(O)Ar}$ ${\bf\ddot{R}}={\bf\Phi}$ methyl, ethyl, benzyl, vinyl; $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}=p$ -X-phenyl with $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H} $ , methyl, methoxy, Cl, $\mathbf{Ar}=o$ -chlorophenyl, etc.), were obtained using this catalyst with moderate-togood enantioselectivities ( $13\mathrm{-}82\%$ ee) and without the formation of sulfones.
## 2.8 Catalysts based on complexes of chiral multifunctional ligands with other metals
A variety of drug-like heteroaryl benzyl(alkyl) sulfoxides 91–93 (Fig. 34) were prepared by oxidation of the corresponding sulfides under mild conditions with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ when bisguanidinium diphosphatobisperoxotungstate, generated in situ using a catalytic amount of silver tungstate $\left({\tt A g}_{2}\mathrm{WO}_{4}\right)$ in the presence of chiral dicationic bisguanidinium salts 94 and identified using Raman spectroscopy and computational studies, were used as a catalyst.76 With optimal reaction conditions, sulfoxides were isolated in good-to-very high yield $(76-96\%$ for 91, $78\mathrm{-}94\%$ for 92, and $76\mathrm{-}96\%$ for 93) and ee’s were always $>80\%$ using 94a. The enantioselective synthesis of (S)-lansoprazole (9b) was accomplished with a yield of $81\%$ and an ee of $90\%$ .
On the other hand, only very low enantioselectivities $(<10\%$ ee) were obtained when enantiopure DENDRI–POM hybrids (S)- $(-)$ -95a and $(R)-(+)\mathbf{-95a}$ (Fig. 35), prepared by ionic coupling of enantiopure 8-armed $n$ -propyl dendritic ammoniums with a catalytically active peroxophosphotungstate trianion $\{\mathrm{PO_{4}}[\mathrm{WO}(\mathrm{O}_{2})_{2}]_{4}\}^{3-},$ , were used for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfide 1b as a model substrate using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant.77
However, bismuth(III) compounds, along with various oxidants and in combination with $(S)$ -BINOL, were found to be an effective catalytic system for the asymmetric oxidation of a series of alkyl aryl sulfides, RSAr ( ${\bf\ddot{R}}=$ methyl, ethyl; $\mathbf{Ar}=p{\mathrm{.}} $ -X-phenyl with $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H}\quad$ , methyl, Br, Cl), and benzyl aryl sulfides, BnSAr $[\mathbf{Ar}=$ phenyl (3g), $p$ -tolyl], with altogether ten examples described.78 The optimized protocol consisting of $10\mathrm{\mol\%}$ ${\bf B}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ , TBHP as oxidant, and ethyl acetate as solvent afforded sulfoxides uncontaminated by their sulfones with high enantioselectivities $78\mathrm{-}98\%$ ee) and in good yields $(81-90\%$ ). It is interesting to note that the chiral PMO SBA-15-type catalysts based on (S)-BINOL applied heterogeneously for the oxidation of 1b afforded 2b with only much lower enantioselectivities $(13-58\%$ ee).79
The asymmetric oxidation of sulfides using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was catalyzed80 by an artificial metalloenzyme as CS formed by the incorporation of a tri-centered cobalt(II)– $^{-2,2^{\prime}}$ -[(1,2-ethanediyl)bis(nitrilopropylidyne)]bisphenol complex (96) into bovine serum albumin (BSA) after determining that $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was superior to other oxidants tested and BSA to the human form of the protein. The structure of the cobalt(II)–Schiff base complex is depicted in Fig. 36. Phenyl vinyl sulfide together with twelve methyl aryl/alkyl sulfides, MeSR $[{\bf R_{\alpha}}=$ phenyl (1b); $o\mathrm{-},\ m\mathrm{-}$ -, and $p$ -chlorophenyl; $m-$ and $p$ -bromophenyl; $p$ -tolyl (1a); $p$ -methoxyphenyl (1k); benzyl, tert-butyl; $n$ -octyl; $n$ -dodecyl], were examined using this hybrid biocatalyst with mixed results after optimizing parameters such as $\mathsf{p H}$ , temperature, oxidants, and substrate, oxidant, and catalyst concentrations using 1b as a model substrate. Conversions $(28\mathrm{-}100\%)$ , yields $(19-98\%)$ , chemoselectivities $(51-100\%)$ , and enantioselectivities $(0{-}87\%$ ee) all varied widely but in selected cases, e.g. substrates $^{1\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b},\mathbf{k},}$ , results were very good with yields of $91\mathrm{-}98\%$ , chemoselectivities of $98\mathrm{-}100\%$ , and ee’s of $50\mathrm{-}69\%$ . In each case the $R$ enantiomer was predominant except for phenyl vinyl sulfoxide where the $s$ enantiomer was predominant and tert-butyl methyl and $n$ -dodecyl methyl sulfoxides where the racemate was produced. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC analysis of the crude reaction mixtures and absolute configurations of the predominant enantiomers determined by comparison of their optical rotations and retention order with literature values.
Another hybrid biocatalyst system was constructed by Ricoux et al.,81 this time using an anionic iron(III)–porphyrin– testosterone conjugate (97, Fig. 37) incorporated into a Streptomyces protein (a neocarzinostatin variant, NCS-3.24) as an expansion of their previous investigations. However, only a slight improvement in the enantioselectivity $10\rightarrow13\%$ ee) for the asymmetric oxidation using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ of sulfide 1b to sulfoxide 2b resulted from this ‘‘Trojan Horse’’ strategy. The $s$ enantiomer was the predominant enantiomer as evaluated by chiral HPLC analysis of the crude reaction mixture. Inclusion of the protein and formation of the 97–NCS-3.24 complex was found to be necessary for induction of optical activity in the product 2b since in the absence of the protein, only racemic material was produced despite the chiral nature of the conjugate. The chemoselectivity of the reaction, nominally $100\%$ , was inconsequential given the exceedingly low substrate conversion of $1.3\%$ , albeit for only a very short reaction duration of two hours. Molecular modeling was used to rationalize the poor enantioselectivity observed but which may nevertheless help facilitate improved design of future hybrid biocatalysts.
Yet another hybrid biocatalyst system was reported82 by Li et al. who assembled an artificial DNA metalloenzyme by the seemingly loose association of a copper(II)–bipyridine complex with natural 21mer human telomeric G-quadruplex DNA $\left[5^{\prime}–(\mathbf{G}_{3}\mathbf{T}_{2}\mathbf{A})_{3}\mathbf{G}_{3}–3^{\prime}\right]$ as the CS. Several ligands for the copper were tested, e.g. 1,10-phenanthroline, 5,6-dimethyl-1,10-phenanthroline, $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bimethyl- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bimethoxy$^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, with the copper(II)– $^{-4,4^{\prime}}$ -bimethyl- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine complex proving superior. Using as test substrate sulfide 1b and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant, after optimization of biotransformation parameters, e.g. $\mathsf{p H}$ , temperature, ratio between G-quadruplex DNA and the copper complex, and concentration of $\mathbf{K}^{+}$ ions, the latter of which were found to be critical for the adoption of the correct conformation of the G-quadruplex DNA, 1b could be converted to the sulfoxide 2b with $99\%$ conversion of 1b, a chemoselectivity of $\sim100\%$ , and an ee for 2b of $56\%$ . Another nine alkyl aryl substrates, $o\mathrm{-}$ and $m$ -chlorophenyl methyl, methyl $m\mathrm{.}$ - and $p$ -tolyl (1a), $p$ -methoxyphenyl methyl (1k), methyl 2-naphthyl, ethyl phenyl (1d), benzyl phenyl $\left(3\mathbf{g}\right)$ , and methyl 2-pyridinyl sulfides, were used to explore the substrate scope with a wide range of conversions from very poor-to-high $(10\mathrm{-}88\%)$ and also with a wide range of enantioselectivities from very poor-tohigh $(6-77\%$ ee). Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC analysis of the crude products while the absolute configurations were not determined.
Finally, a hybrid biocatalyst system was reported83 by Bian et al., also consisting of a multi-center metal complex, by the association of the ${\mathrm{V}}_{8}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ cluster complex, $\mathrm{\big[V_{8}O_{12}(H O^{-})_{4}(C H_{3}O^{-})_{4}}\mathrm{-}$ (1,2-diaminocyclohexane) $\mathbf{_4}]{\cdot}7\mathbf{CH_{3}O H}$ (98, Fig. 38), with various mammalian serum albumins as the CS. Using as test substrate sulfide 1b and BSA, after optimization of biotransformation parameters, e.g. pH, reaction time, and concentrations of catalyst and oxidant, the workers also tested a variety of other albumins (human, pig, rabbit, and sheep) as well as a variety of oxidants $\mathrm{~\boldmath~{~\cal~H}~}_{2}{\bf O}_{2},$ TBHP, NaClO, and $\mathrm{NaIO_{4}}.$ ). Albumins BSA and PSA were determined to be superior with respect to enantioselectivity while TBHP was found to be the superior oxidant also in terms of enantioselectivity as well as yield, conversion, and chemoselectivity. The poorer performances of HSA, RSA, and SSA were reflective of their weaker association with the vanadium complex as indicated from UV-vis spectral results. For 1b along with another seven methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $[\mathrm{Ar}=o\AA,m\AA$ , and $p$ -chlorophenyl; $o\mathrm{-}$ and $p$ -bromophenyl, $p$ -tolyl (1a), $p$ -methoxyphenyl $(\mathbf{1}\mathbf{k})],$ as well as benzyl methyl and phenyl vinyl sulfides used to explore the substrate scope, conversions ranged from fairto-excellent $(71-99\%$ , mostly $>90\%$ ), chemoselectivities ranged from very good-to-excellent $(91-100\%$ ) while isolated yields also ranged from very good-to-excellent $(70-99\%)$ . Enantioselectivities, however, varied wildly from extremely poor-to-good $(1-77\%$ ee). Of note, ee’s generally remained nearly constant over the course of the reactions apart from the notable exception when too much oxidant was used whereby over-oxidation to the sulfone occurred concomitant with a modem of kinetic resolution. Remarkably, the predominant enantiomer was highly variable and depended on the substrate, the albumin, and other conditions, e.g. the oxidant. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC analysis of the crude products while the absolute configurations were determined by comparison of retention order with literature values.
Multifunctional ruthenium complexes 99 containing as ligand a chiral pybox unit functionalized with a chiral bicyclic lactam substituent, along with the parent complex 100, (Fig. 39)
were found84 to be efficient catalysts for the diastereo- and enantioselective oxidation of spirodithiolane–indolones 101 to their corresponding monosulfoxides 102 using dipivaloyloxyiodobenzene $[\mathrm{PhI}(\mathrm{OPiv})_{2}]$ or CHP as oxidant. Under optimal conditions, ten tricyclic 1,3-dithiolanes 101a–i were converted (Scheme 19) into their respective sulfoxides 102 in yields of $52\mathrm{-}71\%$ with $90\%$ ee and with diastereomeric ratios ranging from $83:17$ to $>95.5:4.5$ .
Chiral-at-metal optically active D or $\varLambda$ ruthenium complexes $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{2}(\mathrm{py)}_{2}]^{2+}(\mathrm{bpy}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine, py $\c=$ pyridine) were reacted with alkyl aryl sulfides functionalized at the ortho position with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ [2-(alkylsulfenyl)benzoic acids] or OH [2-((alkylsulfenyl)- phenols)] to yield complexes (Scheme 20) that were then used for the asymmetric synthesis of the sulfoxides. The 2-(alkylsulfinyl)- benzoic acids or (2-(alkylsulfinyl))phenols within the complexes were oxidized with $m$ -chloroperbenzoic acid to yield diastereomeric complexes containing a sulfoxide ligand.85–88 The $\varDelta$ configuration enantioselected for the $R$ enantiomer of the sulfoxide while the L configuration enantioselected the $s$ enantiomer. The sulfoxides were freed from the complexes by treatment with trifluoroacetic acid and sulfoxides $\mathbf{103^{85-87}}$ and $\mathbf{104}^{88}$ prepared by this method are listed in Fig. 40.
## 2.9 Organic oxidation systems
In addition to the metal-based catalytic or stoichiometric oxidation systems presented above, since 2010 there have also been several reports concerning the use of organic oxidation systems for the diastereo- and enantioselective oxidation of sulfides to their corresponding sulfoxides. One report89 concerns the application of a series of $(R)$ -BINOL-derived chiral phosphoric acids 105 and 106 (Fig. 41) as organocatalysts for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides and aldehyde-derived 1,3-dithianes using $50\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant. Under the optimized conditions – $-10\mathrm{mol\%}$ catalyst and 1.5 equivalents of oxidant at $-40{}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ with $\mathbf{CHCl}_{3}$ as solvent – and using phosphoric acid 105f, enantioenriched sulfoxides, $\mathbf{RS(O)Ar}$ ( $\mathbf{\tilde{R}_{\alpha}}=\mathbf{\tilde{\Gamma}}$ methyl, ethyl, benzyl; $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}=p$ -X-phenyl with $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H} $ , methyl, methoxy, Cl, Br, $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}={\boldsymbol{o}}$ -bromophenyl), were obtained in moderate-toexcellent yields $(35-99\%)$ and with moderate-to-good enantio selectivities $(70-82\%$ ee). The series of 2-aryl-1,3-dithianes 1-oxides 108 (Scheme 21) were formed in moderate-to-excellent yields $(60-99\%)$ , with very good diastereomeric excesses (de’s, 76 to ${>}96\%$ ) and moderate-to-good enantioselectivities $56\mathrm{-}82\%$ ee)
from their corresponding 2-aryl-1,3-dithianes 107. For both sets of substrates, the formation of undesirable byproducts, sulfones, disulfones, or disulfoxides, was not observed.
Another publication90 reported almost simultaneously the use of a series of $(R)$ -BINOL-derived chiral iminophosphoric acids 109 (Fig. 42), termed chiral-confined Brønsted acids, as organocatalysts for the oxidation of sulfides using $35\%$ aqueous $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as oxidant. For this protocol under the optimized conditions $-2~\mathrm{mol\%}$ catalyst and 1.05 equivalents oxidant at ambient temperature with cyclohexane as solvent – and using iminophosphoric acid 109b, alkyl aryl sulfoxides, $\mathbf{RS(O)Ar}$ ( ${\bf\ddot{R}}=$ methyl, ethyl, iso-propyl, vinyl; $\mathbf{Ar}={\boldsymbol{o}}\mathbf{-} $ -, $m\cdot$ -, $p$ -chlorophenyl, $p$ -X-phenyl with $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H} $ , methyl, methoxy, $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ , CN), were obtained in excellent yields $(89-96\%)$ and with good-to-excellent enantioselectivities $85{-}99\%$ ee). Only for sulfides with a larger alkyl group than methyl was a small amount of sulfone observed $(5-9\%)$ . Nevertheless, cyclohexyl methyl sulfoxide and dodecyl methyl sulfoxide were still isolated in high yields $96\%$ for both) and with excellent enantioselectivities (94 and $91\%$ ee, respectively). It is worth noting that this protocol afforded $(R)$ -sulindac (82) – taken under consideration91 recently for the treatment of cancer – in $95\%$ overall yield from the corresponding sulfide with an ee of $98\%$ . A large-scale experiment even yielded $_{3\mathrm{~g~}}$ of the product in $97\%$ yield with an ee of $95\%$ .90 It is also interesting to note that insights into the chiral induction for this asymmetric oxidation were gained through analyzing the results from calculations of the stereocontrolling transition states.92
There are also three papers devoted to the use of organocatalysts containing a flavin residue. In the first two publications, the oxidation of methyl aryl and alkyl methyl sulfides with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ catalyzed by flavin–cyclodextrin conjugates 110 and 111 (Fig. 43) was reported.93 It was found that the enantioselectivity $(11-88\%$ ee) depended mainly on the size of the cyclodextrin cavity, the type of flavin unit (alloxazine or isoalloxazine), and the relative orientation of the flavin and cyclodextrin moieties.
The same group also described a series of substituted planar chiral flavinium salts 112 (Fig. 44) as catalysts for enantioselective oxidations with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ .94 Optimization of the structures of the catalysts led to enantioselectivities of up to $61\%$ ee for methyl aryl sulfides and up to $65\%$ ee for tert-butyl methyl sulfide.
Complementing these three reports, an optically active polymer 113 composed of 5-ethyl riboflavinium cations (Fig. 45) was applied95 for the catalyzed asymmetric oxidation using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ of methyl $p$ -tolyl (1a) and methyl 2-naphthyl sulfides and 2-phenyl-1,3-dithiane (107a), to yield their corresponding sulfoxides. However, only very modest yields resulted (39, 40, and $66\%$ , respectively) together with only modest-to-poor enantioselectivities (60, 42, and $13\%$ ee’s, respectively, with ${>}99\%$ de for 107a). Nonetheless, this contrasted well with a monomeric model compound 114 which provided even lower yields (28, 22, and $61\%$ , respectively) together with even poorer enantioselectivities (30, 28, and $2\%$ ee’s, respectively, but still with ${>}99\%$ de for 107a). Methyl 2-naphthyl sulfide and 1a yielded samples predominant in the $s$ enantiomer while 1,3-dithiane 107a yielded samples predominant in the $^{1R,2R}$ enantiomer, Ee’s were determined by chiral HPLC and absolute configurations assigned by comparison of retention times to literature. The enantioselectivities were correlated to the de’s of the 4a-hydroxy derivatives of 113 and 114 (66 and $29\%$ de, respectively, for the two C-4a epimers formed in each case), which presumably reflect the de’s anticipated for the 4a-peroxy derivatives – the active oxidizing agents. The polymeric material 113 adopts a supramolecularly twisted helical structure with excess onehandedness through intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking of the riboflavinium units, and this was held to account for the higher de for the 4a-hydroxy derivative of 113 by chirality transfer from the induced supramolecular helical chirality assisted by the ribityl pendants.
As previously mentioned (see Section 2.1 Titanium tetra-isopropoxide and chiral diols or tetraols), Boyd et al., as part of their study of the biotransformation of 1,2-disulfide substrates (see Section 5. Enzymatic preparation of chiral sulfoxides), used27 a chiral oxaziridine oxidant, $(-){-}(1R)$ -(10-camphorsulfonyl)oxaziridine for the asymmetric oxidation of the cyclic 1,2-disulfides 1,2-dithiane (15) and 1,4-dihydrobenzo-2,3-dithiane (17) to yield thiosulfinates 16 and 18 in excellent yields (89 and $95\%$ , respectively) with poorto-moderate enantioselectivities (52 and $20\%$ ee, respectively) in favor of the $s$ enantiomer (Scheme 6).
## 3. Enzymatic preparation of chiral sulfoxides
In this section, attention is focused on the synthesis of chiral sulfoxides by enzymatic means. The asymmetric preparations described here all involve the oxidation of sulfides to sulfoxides, though three kinetic resolutions that have also been accomplished96–98 by enzymatic means are described in the next Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions, where processes include both the reduction of sulfoxides and hydrolysis within the side chain of a sulfoxide. Without doubt though, many oxidations conducted by enzymatic (and chemical) means probably also include an element of kinetic resolution if over-oxidation to the sulfone also occurs.44 In the preceding Section 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation, four reports80–83 also describe the use of hybrid biocatalysts for the asymmetric oxidation of sulfides to sulfoxides.
The general appeal, as well as the drawbacks, of enzymatic preparations are best considered with respect to the two main methodologies, whole-cell treatment and application of the isolated enzyme, though generally it can be stated that for either approach, the conditions are nominally considered to be mild in comparison to typical chemical transformations and while yields may span a wide range in either case, the usual hope is that attempted enzymatic transformations are highly chemoselective, regiospecific, substrate specific (depending on the systems and aims, this may be as much a drawback as a desired attribute), and stereoselective. Particularly for the latter aspect, this is one of the most appealing attributes of enzymatic transformations but the generally held notion that enzymatic transformations are always completely stereoselective, or nearly so, is far from reality. Moreover, chemoselectivity may not necessarily be a detrimental process as it may lead to an improvement in the enantiomeric excess (ee) by kinetic resolution (see Section 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions). For the treatment of a substrate using whole cells, the process can be cheap and simple to perform, though not necessarily capable of being scaled up conveniently. On the downside, the reaction mixture from a culture broth can be very messy with a multitude of components to separate and the workup can be relatively difficult and furthermore, any manipulation of the sample increases the possibility of SDE via phase transitions or chromatography and potentially as a consequence, mistakes in the determination of the stereochemical outcome can be made. On the other hand, treatment of the substrate with the isolated enzyme can also be simple but the reaction mixture can be much cleaner and easier to work up. Unfortunately, enzymes can be expensive, or require expensive cofactors, particularly in the case of some oxidases, or require intensive effort to acquire the enzyme in purified form. Furthermore, enzymes may be unstable; though if storable, are maintenance-free in comparison to maintaining a seed-culture. All-in-all, there is considerable interest in the asymmetric preparation of sulfoxides by enzymatic means, which has led to a number of reviews, including three recent ones.99–101
Boyd et al. studied102 the biotransformation of benzo[b]thiophene (115a) and its 2-methyl (115b) and 3-methyl (115c) derivatives by whole-cell treatment (Scheme 22) using a set of organisms, Pseudomonas putida NCIMB 8859 expressing naphthalene dioxygenase $\left(\mathrm{NDO}_{2}\right)$ , P. putida mutant 9816/11 expressing $\mathbf{NDO_{1}}$ , Escherichia coli F352V expressing $\mathrm{NDO}_{3}$ , P. putida mutant UV4 expressing toluene dioxygenase (TDO), and $E$ . coli BL21(DE3) cells containing the plasmid pRSET as vector expressing styrene monooxygenase (SMO), a two-component NADH-dependent flavoenzyme obtained from $P.$ putida CA-3. In addition to the desired sulfoxides 116, the reactions generally provided a variety of products, often through a complex series of reactions, e.g. di- and monohydroxy products, dimers, and dehydrated/dehydrogenated/aromatized compounds etc., and very often sulfoxides 116 were produced in only low yields and/or with low ee’s. The problem of complex mixtures and/or unstable products and/or low yields and/or low ee’s due to configurational instability (vide infra) meant that, though interesting from a problem-solving perspective, the processes were not necessarily appealing from a preparative viewpoint.
Applying $\mathbf{NDO_{1-3}}$ ’s and TDO to benzo[b]thiophene (115a) resulted in the formation of tetracyclic products 119tr and 119cis $\left(3:1\right)$ and 120 (Scheme 23), usually as very minor components only $(5-50\%$ relative yields), via the dimerization of benzo[b]thiophene sulfoxide (116a) which could not be isolated, nor could the purported dimer 118 which underwent loss of SO to yield 119tr and 119cis and which then dehydrogenated to yield 120. The tetracyclic products were present with only low ee’s $\left(0-8\%\right)$ due to the configurational instability of 116a and possibly also kinetic resolution. As well, the absolute configurations could not be determined and the compounds were therefore of little interest preparatively. For these four transformations, the sulfone 117 was not observed by way of over-oxidation, or even due to the propensity for these types of structures to $\mathbf{S}/\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ disproportionate. Greater success was achieved with SMO which produced sulfoxide 116a in ca. $97\%$ yield together with ca. $2\%$ of sulfone 117. The sample of 116a was enantioenriched but rapidly racemized within a few hours and was also thermally unstable, either dimerizing or undergoing $\mathbf{S}/\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ disproportionation, over the course of several days. Nevertheless, based on its ECD in comparison to 116b (vide infra) it was tentatively assigned an $R$ configuration. Conversely, 120 appeared to be configurationally stable.
With the methyl derivatives 115b,c, varying results were obtained in terms of producing sulfoxides 116b,c. For 116b, relative yields ranged from abysmal-to-poor $(7-40\%)$ upon treatment with TDO or $\mathsf{N D O}_{1,3}$ while $\mathbf{NDO}_{2}$ and SMO provided relative yields of 90 and $\sim100\%$ , respectively, while sulfones were not detected at all in these transformations. Sulfoxide 116b was relatively stable and the product from TDO treatment was found to have an $\mathrm{ee}>99\%$ based on $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR analysis using a chiral solvating agent with an $R$ configuration based on ECD in comparison to literature. By contrast, sulfoxide 116b obtained from $\mathrm{NDO}_{2}$ treatment in an isolated yield of $26\%$ with an ee of only $56\%$ , moreover, possessed an $s$ configuration by optical rotation. Similarly, sulfoxide 116b obtained in high yield from SMO treatment possessed a low ee of only $3\%$ , again with $s$ configuration by optical rotation. However, sulfoxide 116b, in common with sulfoxide 116a, also only had limited configurational stability, and though better than its parent compound (vide supra), nonetheless a significant degree of racemization of the sample was found to occur after one day in $\mathbf{CHCl}_{3}$ solution. For sulfoxide 116c, relative yields ranged from no production upon treatment with TDO and SMO to poor production with $\mathrm{NDO}_{2}$ $(39\%)$ while $\mathsf{N D O}_{1,3}$ provided relative yields of 90 and $\sim100\%$ , respectively, while sulfones were not detected at all in these transformations. Sulfoxide 116c obtained from $\mathbf{NDO}_{2}$ treatment possessed an ee of $41\%$ with an $R$ configuration based on ECD in comparison to 116b. Finally, the experimentally determined barriers to inversion for 116a–c were $<25$ , 25.1, and $26.4\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1}$ , respectively, $c f.$ to the typical range for sulfoxides of $37{-}42\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1}$ while ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC, ${}^{1}\mathrm{HNMR}$ analysis using the chiral solvating agent $(+)-(S)-1$ -(9-anthryl)- 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol, or optical rotation.
In an expanded examination of substrate scope, the same workers examined103 the biotransformation of a set of seven methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $[\mathbf{Ar}=\mathbf{\partial}$ phenyl (1b), $\scriptstyle O-$ and $p$ -tolyl (1a), $o\mathrm{-}$ - and $p$ -bromophenyl, $\scriptstyle O^{-}$ and $p$ -chlorophenyl] as well as re-examining benzo[b]thiophene (115a) and 2-methylbenzo[b]- thiophene (115b), to their corresponding sulfoxides by wholecell treatment again using $E.$ . coli BL21(DE3) cells containing the plasmid pRSET as vector expressing either wild-type SMO obtained from $P.$ . putida CA-3 or SMO from the engineered P. putida CA-3 mutant R3-11 for comparative studies. Both substrate conversions and chemoselectivities were quantitative $(\sim100\%)$ for both organisms, though utilization rates varied. The wild-type SMO oxidized the $s{\mathrm{.}}$ -acyclic sulfides with comparable rates ${\mathrm{(33-83~\mumoles~\min~^{-1}~g}}$ cell dry weight1), which was much faster than the $s$ -cyclic sulfides 115a,b (8.8 and $7.2\ \upmu\mathrm{moles\min}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}$ cell dry weight1, respectively). Accelerated rates, 1.18–2.80-fold increases, were obtained for the $s$ -acyclic sulfides with the engineered SMO for the respective substrates while the rates for the $s$ -cyclic sulfides 115a,b were even more enhanced at 10.1- and 5.6-fold increases, respectively, rendering them comparable to the rates for the $s$ -acyclic sulfides. However, not only did both organisms produce the same predominant enantiomer for each substrate (six substrates yielded the $R$ enantiomer as predominant while the other three yielded the $s$ enantiomer), they did so with essentially the same enantioselectivities with all pairs of ee’s within $3\%$ error margins, though enantioselectivities varied wildly from exceedingly poor-toexcellent ( $6\%$ ee except for 116a which could not be determined on account of its rapid racemization while the $3\%$ ee value for 116b must be considered an underestimate also on account of its fast – but significantly slower than 116a – racemization103). Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC while the absolute configurations were determined by ECD. A molecular docking study to rationalize both the observed the activities and enantioselectivities was also conducted.
In another grand and expansive study by the same authors, they studied27 the enzyme-catalyzed and chemical asymmetric oxidation (see Section 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation) of 1,2-disulfides. In the first phase, whole-cell treatment using either P. putida mutant UV4 or $P.$ putida NCIMB 8859, the former expressing TDO and the latter NDO of $s$ -acyclic alkyl phenyl substrates, PhSSR ( ${\bf\ddot{R}}={\bf\Phi}$ methyl, ethyl, $n$ -propyl, 121a–c, respectively), and diphenyl 1,2-disulfide (121d) provided unexpected results. Instead of the hoped-for thiosulfinates, other enzymes in the organisms participated in the biotransformations (Scheme 24). Firstly thiol–disulfide oxid/reductase cleaved the disulfide bond to yield thiophenol (122), and then in a reverse manner, form diphenyl 1,2-disulfide (121d), obtained as the major metabolite in $2\mathrm{-}34\%$ yield. Thiol $s{\mathrm{.}}$ -methyl transferase, acting upon 122 (which was also successfully tried directly as a substrate) then provided methyl phenyl sulfide (1b), which, under the actions of the oxidizing enzymes TDO or NDO, was biotransformed to the $R$ and $s$ enantioenriched, respectively, methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2b), albeit in extremely low yields $(2-24\%)$ though with excellent enantioselectivities $(85-97\%$ ee). Substrate conversions were quantitative $(\sim100\%)$ and, together with the poor recovery of material in total, led to the assumption of additional metabolism yielding watersoluble metabolites. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and
Scheme 24 Whole-cell treatment of 1,2-disulfides 121 with either P. putida mutant UV4 or P. putida NCIMB 8859 yielded sulfoxide 2b as the only sulfoxide via additional enzymatic transformations, albeit in extremely low yields though with excellent enantioselectivities.
the absolute configurations determined by comparison of retention orders with literature values.
Another phase of the study entailed27 treatment with isolated enzymes of the cyclic 1,2-disulfides 1,2-dithiane (15) and 1,4-dihydrobenzo-2,3-dithiane (17, Scheme 25) to yield thiosulfinates 16 and 18, the chemical asymmetric oxidation they had accomplished in the same study either with standard Kagan methodology [tert-butyl hydroperoxide (TBHP) with titanium tetra-iso-propoxide and $^{(R,R)}$ -diethyl tartrate] or by applying a chiral oxaziridine oxidant, $(-)\cdot$ -(1R)-(10-camphorsulfonyl)oxaziridine (see Section 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation). Treatment of 15 with a preparation of cyclohexanone monooxygenase (CHMO), isolated from Acinetobacter calcoaceticus NCIB 9871 yielded an enantioenriched sample of 16 in $8\%$ yield and $22\%$ ee predominant in the $R$ enantiomer while 17 was recovered unchanged. With chloroperoxidase (CPO) isolated from Caldariomyces fumago and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , 15 was transformed quantitatively in $\sim100\%$ yield to 16 with $96\%$ ee in favor of the $s$ enantiomer while 18 was obtained in lower yield $(58\%)$ and with lower enantioselectivity $32\%$ ee), also in favor of the $s$ enantiomer, though the enantioselectivity was improved to $47\%$ ee with the same yield upon the inclusion of $20\%$ tert-butanol as co-solvent. Finally, while whole-cell treatment of 15 using either $P.$ . putida mutant UV4 or $P.$ . putida NCIMB 8859 failed to provide 16, whole-cell treatment of 17 using $P.$ putida NCIMB 8859 provided 18 in $11\%$ yield with $9\%$ ee in favor of the $s$ enantiomer. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC analysis while the absolute configuration of (S)-18 was determined by X-ray crystallographic analysis, thereby providing a means to determine the absolute configurations of the 16 enantiomers by comparison of their ECD spectra with those of the 18 enantiomers. In nonpolar solvents, 16 and 18 were found to be configurationally stable over the course of one day at ambient temperature, but in more polar solvents, e.g. water–methanol $\left(1:1\right)$ , under the same conditions, enantiopure 16 was reduced to $17\%$ ee while enantiopure 18 was reduced to $86\%$ ee.
Although the $(S_{\mathrm{S}})$ -sulfoxides of penicillin G (123a), penicillin V (123b), and cephalosporin G can all be readily prepared by direct oxidation, the $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)^{}$ -sulfoxides cannot and require tedious multistep synthesis, thus Jolly et al. developed104 an enzymatic means to produce the $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ -sulfoxides using both whole-cell treatment with the bacterium Bacillus pumilis and by treatment with the catalase–peroxidase enzyme KatG isolated from the bacterium (Scheme 26). Yields were not given but the conversions appeared to be quantitative though in the case of cephalosporin G no product could be isolated from the culture broth due to extensive decomposition. However, using the isolated enzyme, a clean product for the sulfoxide of cephalosporin G was obtained. In all cases, only one stereoisomer was produced and was identified as the stereoisomer with an $R_{\mathrm{{S}}}$ configuration for sulfoxides 124a,b by comparison of their $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR data of their methyl esters to literature and for cephalosporin G by deduction from an analysis of its ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR data.
Scheme 27 Biotransformation of amino acids 125 to $S_{5}$ -configured sulfoxides 126 by IDO-catalyst, the dried-cell preparation of E. coli overexpressing an $\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{I I}})/\mathsf{q}$ -ketoglutarate-dependent dioxygenase from B. thuringiensis 2e2.
Kurina-Sanz et al. tested106 nine different species of filamentous fungi from the Aspergillus genus for their ability to enantioselectively oxidize methyl phenyl (1b) and cyclohexyl methyl sulfides to their corresponding sulfoxides by whole-cell treatment. Performance varied greatly between the different species and was very dependent on the conditions but for the standard screening conditions, conversions ranged from $90\mathrm{-}100\%$ for cyclohexyl methyl sulfide and $7-100\%$ for 1b while enantioselectivities ranged from $0{-}97\%$ ee for cyclohexyl methyl sulfoxide and $0{-}72\%$ ee for 2b. Under these conditions, all species for both substrates exhibited virtually complete chemoselectivity $(\sim100\%)$ . For all non-racemic products, the $R$ enantiomer was predominant. Interestingly, measurement of the ee over the duration of the fermentation revealed that while some species maintained a consistent product ee, for others the ee could change, either increasing or decreasing, with conversion of the substrate. Since the reactions were essentially $100\%$ chemoselective, kinetic resolution/racemization was discounted, though for a case of $90\%$ conversion wherein the product was isolated, only a yield of $21\%$ was obtained. The workers also tested many different biotransformation parameters, e.g. growing/resting cells, oxygenation, culture age, reaction times, biocatalyst–substrate ratios, and use of co-solvents, and found that not one set suited all systems (i.e. fungus and substrate), but the optimal performance for A. japonica strain ICFC 744/11 resulted in a substrate conversion of $\sim100\%$ for cyclohexyl methyl sulfide with complete chemoselectivity $(\sim100\%)$ and an enantioselectivity close to $100\%$ ee. Ee’s were evaluated either by chiral GC (cyclohexyl methyl sulfoxide) or chiral HPLC (2b) and the absolute configurations determined either by comparison of the optical rotation (cyclohexyl methyl sulfoxide) or the retention order (2b) with literature values.
The same group, as part of a comprehensive search for novel Baeyer–Villiger monooxygenase (BVMO)-encoding genes in A. fumigatus Af293, successfully cloned107 one such identified gene and used it to overexpress the recombinant enzyme $\mathbf{BVMO_{Af1}}$ . Through testing different parameters such as temperature, $\mathsf{p H}$ , and the addition of organic co-solvents, it was demonstrated that isolated $\mathbf{BVMO_{Af1}}$ is a robust biocatalyst that can enantioselectively oxidize methyl phenyl (1b) and ethyl benzyl sulfides with $\sim100\%$ conversion in both cases providing moderate-tohigh yields of the corresponding sulfoxides $88\%$ after 3 hours and $56\%$ after 1 hour, respectively, by GC) together with modest-to-high chemoselectivities (92 and $60\%$ , respectively). Notably, both substrates yielded the $s$ enantiomer of the corresponding sulfoxide in ${>}99\%$ ee.
In an impressive experimental and theoretical study of a directed evolution of a BVMO, viz. a thermally robust phenyl acetone monooxygenase (PAMO), Reetz et al. attempted108 to inverse the stereochemical preference from $s$ to $R$ using as model substrate methyl $p$ -methylbenzyl sulfide (127, Scheme 28). From a set of 40 mutant strains and testing them as lysates, they selected the optimum sites to apply iterative saturation mutagenesis on the wild-type PAMO. From a resulting plethora of variants as well as trying different library building strategies, the quadruple mutant ZGZ-2 with point mutations I67Q/P440F/A442N/L443I was found to provide (as an isolated enzyme using NADPH, glucose-6-phosphate, and glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase as the $\mathrm{{NADP^{+}}}$ regeneration system) excellent substrate conversion $(97\%)$ and chemoselectivity $(94\%)$ while the enantioselectivity was also excellent $95\%$ ee) in favor of the $R$ enantiomer. This compared to the wild-type PAMO which also provided good substrate conversion $(69\%)$ , excellent chemoselectivity $(\sim100\%)$ , and very good enantioselectivity $(90\%$ ee), but with the $s$ enantiomer predominant. Deconvolution of the mutant revealed that the single mutations and many of the other smaller combinations of mutations still provided $s$ enantioselectivity, thus alluding to the synergy of the combination of all four mutations to provide effective $R$ enantioselectivity and leading to the construction of a fitness landscape to comprehend the transition from the $s$ enantioselectivity of the wild-type PAMO to the $R$ enantioselectivity of the quadruple mutant. An in-depth molecular docking study to rationalize the observed enantioselectivities was also conducted. For the substrate scope, a set of five additional methyl aryl/alkyl sulfides, MeSR $[{\bf R}=$ benzyl, phenyl (1b), $p$ -chlorophenyl, $p$ -bromophenyl, $n$ -butyl], and ethyl phenyl sulfide (1d) were tested for conversion to their corresponding sulfoxides using as isolated enzymes the wild-type PAMO as well as the optimized mutant ZGZ-2 and a number of other mutants. For the quadruple mutants ZGZ-2 and $^{-1}$ , substrate conversions were very goodto-excellent $(88-99\%)$ , chemoselectivities were good-to-excellent $(78\mathrm{-}100\%)$ while enantioselectivities were excellent $(91-97\%$ ee in favor of the $R$ enantiomer). Interestingly, for the wild-type PAMO, while chemoselectivities were very good-to-excellent $\left(86-100\%\right)$ , substrate conversions $(35-99\%)$ and enantioselectivities $9\mathrm{-}99\%$ ee in favor of the $s$ enantiomer generally) varied wildly, and, unexpectedly, with even inversed enantioselectivity being observed in one case for methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2b) where the $R$ enantiomer was predominant in $42\%$ ee. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and the absolute configurations were determined by comparison of retention order with literature or authentic reference standards.
In another case of $R$ vs. $s$ selectivity, Elkin et al. examined109 the biotransformation of methyl phenyl (1b), ethyl phenyl (1d), methyl $p$ -tolyl (1a), and benzyl methyl $\bf(1n)$ sulfides to their corresponding sulfoxides $^{2\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b},\mathbf{d},\mathbf{n}}$ (Scheme 29) by whole-cell treatment with either Gordonia terrae IEGM 136 or Rhodococcus rhodochrous IEGM 66 bacteria. Interestingly, while $G$ . terrae
IEGM 136 yielded products enantioenriched in the $R$ enantiomer, R. rhodochrous IEGM 66 yielded products enantioenriched in the $s$ enantiomer. Substrate conversions were very good-toexcellent $77\mathrm{-}100\%$ for both microbes) and chemoselectivities were similarly also very good-to-excellent $84-95\%$ and $78–100\%$ , respectively) while enantioselectivities were good-to-excellent $(77-95\%$ ee and $68\mathrm{-}100\%$ ee, respectively). Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and the absolute configurations were determined by comparison of the optical rotations with literature values. Optimization of biotransformation parameters, e.g. pH, oxygenation, temperature, and substrate concentrations, was conducted but the microbes were largely indifferent to changes except for the concentration of the test substrate, 1b, which exhibited notable toxic effects above $\mathbf{4\mM}$ .
In their examination of the biotransformation of a set of methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $[\mathbf{Ar}=$ phenyl (1b), $p$ -tolyl (1a), $p{\cdot}$ -chlorophenyl, $p$ -bromophenyl], to their corresponding sulfoxides by whole-cell treatment for the Rieske non-heme dioxygenase nitrobenzene dioxygenase (NBDO) obtained from Comamonas sp. JS765 using E. coli BL21(DE3) cells containing the plasmid pET Duet 1, Fishman et al. also managed110 to improve either the activity and/or the enantioselectivity by protein engineering via saturation mutagenesis at five different residues in the active site. For the wild-type enzyme, enantioselectivities were 71, 4.9, 4.9, and $2.2\%$ ee, all in favor of $R$ enantiomer, which were easily surpassed by variants from the generated libraries with maximal ee’s of 87, 77, 73, and $64\%$ , respectively, for particular substrates only. Product formation was also improved markedly with 2.4-, 4.8-, 7.4-, and 17-fold changes attained for one variant. Improvements for both aspects could be obtained by the combination of two favorable mutations, e.g. 1.7-, 4.6-, 7.1-, and 26.7-fold changes in product formation, respectively, and ee’s of 82, 65, 68, and $68\%$ , respectively. However, it should be noted that product formation and enantioselectivities varied wildly and not necessarily in concert and not consistently across the set of substrates. Even opposite enantioselectivities for the $s$ enantiomer of up to $80\%$ ee could be obtained from some variants. For the mainly short duration experiments, chemoselectivities were quantitative $(\sim100\%)$ , but with prolonged experiments sulfones could be detected. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and absolute configurations were determined either by comparison of retention times by chiral HPLC with authentic enantiomers for 1a or by comparison of their retention orders on chiral GC with literature values for the other three compounds. A feature of the work was the in-depth molecular docking study to rationalize both the observed activities and the enantioselectivities.
Li et al. developed111 an ionic liquid co-solvent biphasic system to improve both the production and the enantioselectivity in the biotransformation of another set of methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $[\mathbf{Ar}=\mathbf{\partial}$ phenyl (1b), $p$ -tolyl (1a), $p$ -methoxyphenyl $(\mathbf{1}\mathbf{k})$ , $p$ -fluorophenyl], and ethyl phenyl sulfide (1d) to their corresponding sulfoxides by whole-cell treatment using resting E. coli BL21(DE3) cells containing double plasmids pET Duet 1 and pRSF Duet 1 co-expressing a three-component P450pyr monooxygenase obtained from Sphingomonas sp. HXN-200 and glucose dehydrogenase, respectively, for recycling of the necessary cofactor $\mathrm{{NAD}(P)H}$ via oxidation of glucose. Using as test substrate 1b, they evaluated the wild-type enzyme and four mutants and found that mutant P450pyrI83H-GDH proved superior, although all were comparable in terms of enantioselectivity (ca. $60\text{\textperthousand}$ ee). They also tested four ionic liquids as co-solvents with trihexyltetradecylphosphoniumbis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide, $\big[\mathrm{P}_{6,6,6,14}\big]^{+}\big[\mathrm{NTf}_{2}\big]^{-}$ being the only successful one. Ultimately it was the use of $\big[\mathrm{P}_{6,6,6,14}\big]^{+}\big[\mathrm{NTf}_{2}\big]^{-}$ $25\%$ v/v) that proved effective for the improvements, particularly with respect to combating substrate inhibition at higher concentrations, the highly beneficial effect with respect to yield and enantioselectivity due to the demonstrated toxicity and inhibition of sulfoxidation by both substrates and product sulfoxides. With the use of $\mathbf{\big[P_{6,6,6,14}\big]^{+}}[\mathbf{NTf}_{2}]^{-}$ , yields of the corresponding sulfoxides could be increased by $2.1{-}5.2{-}$ fold with an average of 4-fold for the five substrates. Enantioselectivities could be increased by 8–27 percentage points with an average of 16 percentage points for the five substrates (from a range of $35\mathrm{-}88\%$ ee to $62\mathrm{-}99.5\%$ ee). In all cases, the $R$ enantiomer was preferentially produced. Chemoselectivities were quantitative $(\sim100\%)$ and sulfones could not be detected, even after incubation for one day. Scaled up preparations $\mathrm{(50~mL)}$ of 2b,d and methyl $p$ -fluorophenyl sulfoxide were successful with ee’s of 99, 98, and $96\%$ , respectively, though yields were poor $(19-21\%)$ ) due to the high substrate concentrations employed $\mathbf{\left(30{-}60\ m M\right)}$ . Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC analysis of the crude reaction mixtures and absolute configurations were determined by comparison of optical rotations for 2b,d and methyl $p$ -fluorophenyl sulfoxide with literature values.
Turner et al. also examined112 the biotransformation of a set of methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $[\mathbf{Ar}=\mathbf{\partial}$ phenyl (1b), $p$ -tolyl (1a), $p$ -methoxyphenyl (1k), $p$ -bromophenyl, $p$ -fluorophenyl, $p$ -nitrilephenyl], to their corresponding sulfoxides by whole-cell treatment for the catalytically self-sufficient cytochrome P450 RhF (RhF) (CYP116B2) obtained from Rhodococcus sp. NCIMB 9784 using $E.$ . coli BL21(DE3) cells containing the plasmid $\ensuremath{\mathbf{P}}450$ RhF/LIC. Substrate conversions were excellent $(96\mathrm{-}100\%)$ and chemoselectivities were quantitative $(\sim100\%)$ while enantioselectivities were excellent (89 to ${>}95\%$ ee) for all substrates except for 1k which only provided $65\%$ ee. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and it was ascertained that the ee’s remained constant over the duration of the experiments. The S enantiomer was predominant for ${\bf1}{\bf a},{\bf b},{\bf k}$ as proven by comparison of their retention orders on chiral HPLC with literature values while for the other three compounds, the absolute configuration was assumed to follow the same trend as ${\bf1}\mathbf{a},{\bf b},{\bf k}$ with the $s$ enantiomer also predominant.
As part of a wide examination in terms of substrate scope, Yu et al. also examined113 the biotransformation of ethyl phenyl (1d), $p$ -methoxyphenyl methyl (1k), $p$ -chlorophenyl methyl, and methyl 2-thienyl sulfides to their corresponding sulfoxides by isolated-enzyme treatment using the catalytically self-sufficient cytochrome P450 monooxygenase LaMO (CYP116B4) obtained from Labrenzia aggregata IAM 12614 following its identification by genomic data mining and substrate testing. After cloning, the protein was overexpressed using $E.$ . coli to enable isolation of the pure protein $\mathbf{P450}_{L a\mathbf{MO}}$ , which could then be used for substrate biotransformation requiring only $\mathbf{NAD(P)H}$ cofactor for substrate turnover. Following limited optimization testing of the biotransformation parameters, e.g. pH and temperature, for $\mathbf{P450}_{L a\mathbf{MO}}$ using $p$ -chlorophenyl methyl sulfide as one of the test substrates (and which was also used for the substrate testing of the original set of twenty putative monooxygenases for identification of enzymatic activity), substrate conversions for the sulfides ranged from modest-to-excellent $(58-98\%)$ while enantioselectivities were excellent (91 to ${>}99\%$ ee) for all substrates except for 1k which only provided $70\%$ ee. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and the $s$ enantiomer was determined to be predominant for all compounds by comparison of their retention orders on chiral HPLC with literature values or authentic reference standards.
He et al. also examined114 the biotransformation of a similar set of methyl aryl sulfides, MeSAr $[\mathbf{Ar}=\mathbf{\partial}$ phenyl (1b), $p$ -tolyl (1a), $p$ -chlorophenyl, $p$ -fluorophenyl], to their corresponding sulfoxides by whole-cell treatment using resting cells of the bacterium Rhodococcus sp. CCZU10-1 with sulfide monooxygenase activity. Optimization of biotransformation parameters, e.g. pH, temperature, and cell and substrate concentrations, was conducted using as test substrate 1b wherein similar problems as before were encountered in that at concentrations above $10~\mathrm{\mM}$ , toxicity/enzyme inhibition limited progress of the reaction with yields quickly dropping down from $92\%$ . The authors overcame the problem of limited substrate concentration by testing a variety of organic co-solvents in a biphasic system whereby they found that $n$ -octane $\left(15\%~\mathrm{v/v}\right)$ provided superior results and methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2b) could be formed at a concentration of $55~\mathrm{~mM}$ . Moreover, using a feed-batch process, a reaction concentration of $118\ \mathrm{mM}$ 2b could be attained with an isolated yield of $67\%$ . What was remarkable was that the enantioselectivity was exceptional at $>99.9\%$ ee and was invariant irrespective of the applied conditions, including all substrates tested. Additionally notable was that $\sim100\%$ chemoselectivity was also always attained. Although the yields for the four compounds was only modest-to-good under the optimized conditions $(50\mathrm{-}79\%)$ , this was more than made up for by the unsurpassed enantioselectivity of the biotransformation. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and it was ascertained that the ee’s remained constant over the duration of the experiments. The absolute configurations of the sulfoxides of all four substrates was determined to be $s$ by comparison of their optical rotations with literature values.
Chen et al. also examined115 biotransformation by wholecell treatment, again using resting cells, of the soil bacterium P. monteillii by first screening a set of five different strains using as test substrate methyl phenyl sulfide (1b) with the key criterion being similar conversion $(99\%)$ at high concentration $\left(30\mathrm{\mM}\right)$ as was obtained at low concentration $\left(10\mathrm{\mM}\right)$ . The selected strain, P. monteillii CCTCC M2013683 and 1b were used for the optimization of biotransformation parameters, e.g. pH, temperature, cell and substrate concentrations, and the use of co-solvents, with, in contrast to the previous study, the latter proving mostly ineffective with yields either depressed or almost non-existent. The standout features were the persistent chemoselectivity at $\sim100\%$ and the enantioselectivity, which remained essentially invariant at $99\%$ ee and largely irrespective of the applied conditions, the exceptions being the use of co-solvents in two cases where the ee dropped to 98 and $95\%$ ee and the cultivations at higher temperatures where the ee also dropped to $98\%$ ee. It was ascertained by sampling over time that the ee remained otherwise constant during an extended run. Substrate scope was explored by testing other alkyl aryl sulfides, viz. 2-chloroethyl phenyl, allyl phenyl (1h), methyl 2-naphthyl, and methyl $m$ -chlorophenyl sulfides. Conversions ranged from fair-toexcellent $(69-99\%)$ with the exception of methyl $m$ -chlorophenyl sulfide $(54\%)$ while enantioselectivities also ranged from fair-toexcellent $(63-99\%$ ee). Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and the absolute configurations of the sulfoxides of all five substrates was determined to be $R$ by comparison of their optical rotations with literature values. Rather interestingly, in a follow-up molecular biology study, it was found116 that the growth conditions (Scheme 30) of the bacterial strain strongly affected the catalytic properties of the microbe. For the previous study, both the growth medium (M9) and the supplied toluene, used for both an energy and as a carbon source, were critical for the resulting sulfoxidations as using other growth media – with or without toluene – only yielded poor results in comparison. Thus enzyme expressions were adapted in response to the conditions and this was confirmed by the transcriptome profiling and DEG analysis which revealed that protein synthesis, energy metabolism, and the biosynthesis of metabolites were significantly changed. Eight enzymes were identified as potential candidates responsible for the sulfoxidations.
In their quest for typical mammalian metabolites of drug molecules, Fraaije et al. tested117 a collection of microbial flavoprotein monooxygenases (FMO’s, a subset of BVMO’s) against the model substrate $m$ -aminophenyl methyl sulfide (129) and the anthelmintic (pro)drugs albendazole (130a) and fenbendazole (130b), the former bearing structural similarity to the latter two (Fig. 46). The collection included PAMO and its mutant $\mathbf{PAMO_{M446G}}$ from Thermobifida fusca; a cyclohexanone monooxygenase $\mathrm{(CHMO_{Ac})}$ from Acinetobacter calcoaceticus; $\mathrm{\mathbf{BVMO_{Rj4}}}$ , $\mathrm{\mathbf{BVMO_{Rj24}}}$ , and $\mathbf{FMO}_{\mathrm{RjE}}$ (a so-called type II FMO) from Rhodoccocus jostii; and $\mathbf{BVMO_{Mt1}}$ from the fungus Myceliophthora thermophile. Enzymes were overexpressed using $E.$ coli cells with either plasmid pCRE 2 or pET_SUMO as vectors and extracts from harvested cells were used as lysates for the biotransformations, together with phosphite, NADPH, and phosphite dehydrogenase to regenerate the reduced coenzyme. All enzymes were able to enantioselectively oxidize 129 with two of the enzymes producing the S enantiomer with ee’s of 66 and ${>}99.5\%$ while conversely the other five enzymes produced the $R$ enantiomer with ee’s ranging from $36\mathrm{-}88\%$ . Chemoselectivities were excellent $(98\mathrm{-}100\%)$ and conversions for $\mathbf{BVMO_{Mt1}}$ and $\mathrm{\mathbf{B}V M O_{R j24}}$ were 97 and $72\%$ respectively. However, only $\mathbf{CHMO_{Ac}}$ $32\%$ ee), $\mathbf{BVMO_{Mt1}}$ $(55\%$ ee), and $\mathrm{\mathbf{BVMO_{Rj24}}}$ $95\%$ ee) were able to enantioselectively oxidize 130a, the former two yielding the $R$ enantiomer with the latter the $s$ enantiomer. Yields for $\mathbf{BVMO_{Mt1}}$ and $\mathrm{\mathbf{B}V M O_{R j24}}$ were 25 and $55\%$ , respectively, while chemoselectivities were excellent $(>99\%)$ for all three enzymes. Only $\mathbf{BVMO_{Mt1}}$ was able to sulfoxidize 130b but could do so with an ee of $96\%$ in favor of the $s$ enantiomer. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and the absolute configuration of $m$ -aminophenyl methyl sulfoxide was determined by ECD analysis and by comparison of its optical rotation with literature while for the sulfoxides of the (pro)drugs 130a,b the absolute configurations were determined by comparison of retention order with literature.
In a similar approach screening microbes rather than substrates, de Oliveira et al. found118 that of six fungi, Nigrospora sphaerica (Sacc.) E. W. Mason SS67, Papulaspora immersa Hotson SS13, Pestalotiopsis foedans VR8, Glomerella cingulata VA1, Fusarium oxysporum SS50, and Mucor rouxii NRRL 1894, tested against the sulfide anthelmintic prodrug albendazole (130a), all could enantioselectively oxidize it to its pharmacologically active sulfoxide metabolite, ricobendazole (131, Fig. 47), with varying degrees of success. M. rouxii NRRL 1894 was the most efficient and yields from its fermentation could reach ca. $30\%$ with an ee of $90\%$ in favor of the $(+)$ enantiomer (configuration ${\boldsymbol{R}}^{117}$ ). Of note, ee’s were observed to vary by as much as $25\%$ across the duration of the fermentations depending on the organism. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC although the absolute configuration of the $(+)$ enantiomer was not determined at the time of the work. Significantly, the $(+)$ enantiomer was determined to be the only bioactive form in comparison to the $(-)$ enantiomer or even the racemate against the parasite Trichinella spiralis in a murine model – a well-established means for determining anthelmintic activity.
Jiang et al. were also interested119 in using biotransformation to procure the desirable enantiomer of a chiral sulfoxide drug, viz. $(R)$ -modafinil (55), by oxidation of the corresponding sulfide. To this end they applied a CPO in pure form isolated from the fungus $C.$ . fumago and while enantioselectivities were excellent at $97.3\%$ ee and essentially invariant under all conditions applied, the yield using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as the oxidant was extremely poor at $5.4\%$ , though it could be increased to $11.5\%$ using TBHP in place of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ . Of note, the chemoselectivity appeared to be effectively $100\%$ . To overcome this limitation of poor yield, optimization of the biotransformation parameters including pH, reaction time, oxidant and enzyme concentrations, and use of co-solvents was conducted. The latter proved most effective and of the variety of ionic liquids, quaternary ammonium salts, and polyhydroxy compounds tested, 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium bromide $(10\%\ \mathrm{v/v})$ proved superior leading to an almost fourfold increase in the yield to $41\%$ . Another advantage of the use of the CPO was the low concentration ${\bf\left(13\mu M\right)}$ of the enzyme required to effect oxidation. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and the absolute configuration of 55 was determined to be $R$ by comparison of its HPLC retention order with literature.
## 4. Kinetic and thermodynamically driven resolutions
There are now four general approaches120 to the kinetic resolution of chiral sulfoxides (Scheme 31), viz. oxidation to the sulfone, reduction to the sulfide, modification of a side chain, and the latest addition to the repertoire, imidation to the sulfoximine. Oxidation and reduction both result in transformed substrates that are achiral, so it is the unwanted sulfoxide enantiomer that necessarily has to be the one that is transformed while leaving the desired enantiomer to be recovered unaltered in these approaches. Kinetic resolution by oxidation (and for systems which can be reversible, also reduction) may be much more accessible than realized and the conjecture has been made44 that if a system can induce stereoselectivity in the sulfide $\mid\rightarrow\mid$ sulfoxide oxidation, it may then be applicable for kinetic resolution by the sulfoxide $\mid\rightarrow\mid$ sulfone oxidation step. The basis for this conjecture is the observation that many asymmetric oxidations seemed to involve a component of kinetic resolution. Imidation and side-chain modification both yield transformed substrates which are chiral, so the desired sulfoxide enantiomer can either be the unchanged enantiomer that remains or the transformed one, which in the latter case may either be the final objective of the synthesis or else transformation back to the starting structure may be required. Irrespective, for the imidation and side-chain modification approaches, as a result of the applied process it is possible that not only the primarily desired sulfoxide enantiomer is obtained, but another chiral product that is also enantioenriched is obtained as well. Thus in principle, both enantiomers of the sulfoxide might be accessible. The recent examples discussed below encompass all four of these approaches. Three of the described reports96–98 involve enzymatic transformations but are placed here rather than in the preceding section dealing with enzymatic transformations (Section 3. Enzymatic preparation of chiral sulfoxides). Thermodynamic resolution may also parallel any of these approaches but the example presented here involves diastereomeric complex formation with a chiral-at-metal iridium complex.
As already noted previously (see Section 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation), Galindo et al. reported72 the molybdenum-catalyzed asymmetric oxidation of methyl phenyl sulfide (1b, Scheme 32) to methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2a) using an imidazolium-based dicarboxylic acid derived from L-valine as the chiral selector.
While selectivity for the sulfoxide 2b over the sulfone 6b and conversion of the starting sulfide 1b were generally good under a variety of tested conditions (solvent, temperature, oxidant, etc.) with optimum values of 95 and $94\%$ , respectively, enantiomeric excesses (ee’s) of only $40\%$ as determined by chiral HPLC in favor of the known $R$ enantiomer were obtained for 2b. However, utilizing a 1.6-fold excess of the oxidant $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}\right)$ in the reaction, concomitant kinetic resolution enabled improvement of the product sulfoxide 2b ee to a respectful $83\%$ with an excellent yield (in consideration of only $50\%$ of the starting material is available as the desired enantiomer) of a little less than $40\%$ based on the starting sulfide 1b. Specific testing of the kinetic resolution of racemic methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2b) using the oxidizing system in question was not conducted however but must surely be effective given these results. The results also fully support the aforementioned conjecture regarding the application of known asymmetric oxidizing systems for kinetic resolution.
From their investigation into asymmetric oxidation using Fe(salen)-based MOF’s (see Section 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation), Cui et al. queried59 (Scheme 33) whether either the Fe(salen)-based MOF 77 $\mathrm{\{[{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{{FeL}_{2})_{2}{O}(\mathrm{{BPDC})_{2}]{\cdot}\mathrm{{6DMA\cdot8H_{2}O\}}}}}}$ or the free catalyst $\big[\big(\mathrm{FeL}_{2}\big(\mathrm{OAc}\big)\big)\big]$ , could effect the kinetic resolution of racemic iso-propyl phenyl sulfoxide (2e). The results – a moderate ee of $60\%$ was obtained in both cases as determined by chiral HPLC – confirmed their postulate that the gain in ee of the product 2e from the asymmetric oxidation of iso-propyl phenyl sulfide (1e) with extended reaction times was a result of kinetic resolution (together of course with the concomitant production of sulfone 6e). Although yields for the kinetic resolution were not given and must naturally be lower, use of the free catalyst attained the level of $60\%$ ee three-fold faster than the MOF catalyst system due to accessibility factors. With the $^{S,S}$ -configured catalyst, $s$ -enantioenriched products resulted.
From their investigation into asymmetric oxidation using $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathbf{OTf})_{2}$ with $36\%$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and the salan-like ligand 80b containing an additional porphyrin-inspired chiral 4,5-dihydro-1,3-oxazole substituent (see Section 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation), Gao et al. also anticipated63 that since the oxidations of the sulfides to their corresponding sulfoxides were highly enantioselective and therefore kinetic resolution of sulfoxides by further oxidation to sulfones should be successful, kinetic resolution of sulfoxides by further oxidation to sulfones was attempted. Both methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2b) and omeprazole (9a) were examined, with the former showing that the $R$ enantiomer was preferentially oxidized to the sulfone 6b 7.7 times faster than the $s$ enantiomer providing $s$ -enantioenriched 2b in $34\%$ yield and $90\%$ ee. The $R$ enantiomer of omeprazole (9a) was preferentially oxidized to the corresponding sulfone 7.1 times faster than the $s$ enantiomer providing enantioenriched esomeprazole (9a) in $39\%$ yield and $89\%$ ee. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC and absolute configurations determined by comparison of HPLC retention order with literature.
Likewise in their follow-up study using the same oxidation system and testing various additives (see Section 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation), Gao et al. also conducted64 a kinetic resolution using their optimized system [catalytic amounts of adamantoic acid $(20\mathrm{mol\%})$ in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}/$ iso-propanol ( $\operatorname{i}:2$ ratio), $0.5~\mathrm{mol\%}$ ligand 80b, $0.5\ \mathrm{mol\%}$ $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{OTf})_{2}$ , and 0.7 equivalents of $50\%\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}]$ on methyl phenyl (2b) and benzyl $o$ -chlorophenyl sulfoxides. Interestingly, whereas in the previous study63 the $R$ enantiomer was reported to be preferentially oxidized to the sulfone 6b, in this system it was reported that the $s$ enantiomer was preferentially oxidized to the sulfone 6b 7.3 times faster than the $R$ enantiomer providing $R\mathrm{.}$ -enantioenriched 2b in $33\%$ yield and $90\%$ ee. For benzyl $o$ -chlorophenyl sulfoxide, the $s$ enantiomer was preferentially oxidized to the corresponding sulfone 11.0 times faster than the $R$ enantiomer providing $R$ -enantioenriched sulfoxide in $32\%$ yield and $98\%$ ee. Ee’s were evaluated by chiral HPLC.
As part of their ligand screening of a large set of camphor-based Schiff-base ligands for asymmetric oxidation using ${\mathrm{VO}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{2}$ (see Section 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation), Chen et al. examined44 (Scheme 34) the kinetic resolution of racemic ethyl 2-naphthyl sulfoxide (2l). Although the yield of $29\%$ was poor for the optimal result in terms of enantioselective synthesis, the ee of $94\%$ as determined by chiral HPLC was excellent with the predominant $s$ enantiomer assigned by comparison of its optical rotation to literature.
Dong et al. reported121 the dynamic kinetic resolution of aryl allylic sulfoxides 132a–f (Scheme 35) by side-chain modification. Mislow [2,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement was responsible for the racemization of the substrates 132a–f while the kinetic resolution itself was effected by enantioselective hydrogenation of the allylic side chain to an $n$ -propyl chain catalyzed by a rhodium bisphosphine complex. The $^{\mathbf{\alpha}_{S,S}}$ enantiomer of the bisphosphine ligand preferentially reduced the $R$ enantiomer of the sulfoxides 132a–f to their corresponding aryl $n$ -propyl sulfoxides 133a–f. The rhodium catalyst also enhanced the rate of racemization and thus served a dual role though for racemization it was only effective in polar solvents. For efficient dynamic kinetic resolution, the rate of hydrogenation was required to be controlled relative to the rate of racemization by managing the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ pressure. Optimization of the conditions (solvent, catalyst loading, $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ pressure, etc.) provided yields that varied from a high of $84\%$ for 133d to a low of $53\%$ for 133f and enantioselectivities determined by chiral SCF chromatography that ranged from $84\mathrm{-}88\%$ ee for 133a, 133c, 133d, and 133f though an ee of only $50\%$ was obtained for the unsubstituted 133b. The kinetics and mechanism of the reaction were well studied.
A kinetic resolution by side-chain modification reminiscent of Dong et al.121 was reported122 by Vidal-Ferran et al. using in similar fashion a rhodium phosphine–phosphite complex to hydrogenate a $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ double bond to an ethyl group in aryl vinyl sulfoxides $\mathbf{134a-h}$ (Scheme 36). Although dynamic kinetic resolution was not in effect, both the unreacted enantiomers $\mathbf{134a-h}$ and the transformed enantiomers 135a–h could be obtained in good-to-high yields $27\mathrm{-}40\%$ and $28\mathrm{-}40\%$ , respectively) with high enantioselectivities as determined by chiral HPLC $98\mathrm{-}99\%$ ee and $80{-}97\%$ ee, respectively) at the optimal conditions determined for each substrate 134a–h and product $\mathbf{135a-h}$ .
Scheme 36 Structures of racemic aryl vinyl sulfoxides $134a-h$ subjected to kinetic resolution by side-chain modification. For the benzyl compound 134h, due to a change in atomic priority the configuration of the unreacted enantiomer is denoted as $R$ although the spatial arrangement of the substituents in the product is the same as for the other compounds $134a-9$
The conditions tested included the phosphine–phosphite ligand (two different gross structures with two epimers of each), solvent, and the reaction time. Using the optimal ligand with an $\mathbf{\xi}_{R,S,S_{a}}$ configuration preferentially transformed the $R$ enantiomer in each of $\mathbf{134a-h}$ , though use of the epimeric ligand of configuration ${R,S},{R_{\mathrm{a}}}$ yielded the $s$ enantiomer. For the benzyl compound 134h, although the spatial arrangement of the substituents in the product is the same as for the products of the other compounds $\mathbf{134a-g}$ , due to a change in atomic priority, the configuration of the unreacted enantiomer is denoted as $R$ (as too is the transformed enantiomer, i.e. both are $R!$ ). Absolute configurations of 134a–h and 135a–h were determined by comparison of optical rotations to reported data or by analogy to the other compounds examined. The mechanism of the reaction was well studied.
The kinetic resolution of the previously unknown sulfoxides phenyl acetoxymethyl sulfoxide (136a, Scheme 37) and $p$ -tolyl acetoxymethyl sulfoxide (136b) by side-chain modification has been reported96 by Kiełbasin´ski et al. by way of enzymecatalyzed hydrolysis of the acetyl group. The resulting hydroxymethyl sulfoxides 137a and 137b were unstable leaving the unreacted enantiomer as the obtainable product. A variety of enzymes were tested in either buffer or iso-propyl solutions. For phenyl acetoxymethyl sulfoxide (136a), the $s$ enantiomer was always left in excess irrespective of the enzyme or solvent while for $p$ -tolyl acetoxymethyl sulfoxide (136b) the excess enantiomer left unreacted depended on the enzyme used though independent of the solvent system. For optimal conditions, reasonable yields of $30{-}34\%$ could be obtained with ee’s in the range $90\mathrm{-}98\%$ as determined by ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR using a chiral solvating agent (CSA). The absolute configurations of the sulfoxides were determined by ECD spectra in comparison to the ECD spectra of closely related analogs.
Parvulescu et al. reported97 a two-stage method for the preparation of enantiopure sulfoxides by deracemization using Escherichia coli. In the first stage, racemic sulfoxide was subjected to $E.$ . coli cells whereby the $R$ enantiomer was selectively reduced to the corresponding sulfide while the $s$ enantiomer remained unreacted. The resulting sulfide was then oxidized chemoselectively back to the sulfoxide (as a racemate) with a heterogeneous $\mathrm{Ta}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}{\cdot}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ catalyst using $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to provide fresh substrate for resolution. Three aryl methyl sulfoxides, viz. methyl phenyl (2b), methyl $p$ -tolyl (2a), and methyl mesityl sulfoxide, were examined by this process and after one cycle, the best obtainable enantioselectivity under optimal conditions was $62\%$ ee for 2a with a conversion of $38\%$ while the enantioselectivity for 2b was much lower (ca. $25\%$ ee) and the enantioselectivity for methyl mesityl sulfoxide was negligible $(1\%$ ee). The process however, could be repeated, and after three cycles the ee for 2a was able to be increased to $98\%$ together with a yield of $56\%$ for the $s$ enantiomer. Ee’s were determined by chiral HPLC and the absolute configuration of 2b assigned by comparison of its optical rotation to literature. This sequential deracemization strategy may be a viable complement to other kinetic resolutions by overcoming the maximum-yield limitation of $50\%$ akin to dynamic kinetic resolution.
Rather than whole-cell treatment, Bernhardt et al. utilized98 an isolated molybdenum enzyme, respiratory DMSO reductase from the bacterium Rhodobacter capsulatus, to effect the kinetic resolution of three sulfoxides, viz. methyl phenyl (2b), methyl $p$ -tolyl (2a), and phenyl vinyl sulfoxide by electrochemical reduction to their corresponding sulfides. A hexaaminecobalt coordination compound, $\mathrm{[Co(}t r a n s\mathrm{-}\mathrm{diammac)](ClO_{4})_{3}}$ , in its divalent oxidation state was employed as the mediator of electron transfer between the working electrode and the DMSO reductase to continually reactivate the enzyme after turnover. Actual yields were not given, but it was possible to obtain milligram quantities of sulfoxide after 1–3 hours of reaction on a scaled-up system and although the yields of 2b and 2a might only amount to $10{-}20\%$ , the final remaining amounts of 2b and 2a were effectively enantiopure ( $\sim100\%$ ee). Less enantiodiscrimination was observed for the more bulky phenyl vinyl sulfoxide and the best enantioselectivity observed was $71\%$ ee, thus alluding to the size-limited substrate acceptance of the enzyme. Indeed, no catalytic activity was exhibited when diphenyl sulfoxide was employed as the substrate. For all three compounds, the $R$ enantiomer was predominant where ee’s were determined by chiral HPLC and the absolute configuration of 2a was assigned from the retention time of an authentic sample of $(R)-(+)-2\mathbf{a}$ with the absolute configurations of 2b and phenyl vinyl sulfoxide based on the assumption that their enantiomers follow the same retention order by HPLC as those of 2a. The preferential reduction of the $s$ enantiomer was readily rationalized based on the X-ray crystallographic structure of the enzyme.
Bolm et al. reported120 the kinetic resolution of a series of aryl alkyl sulfoxides 138a–j (Scheme 38) and a dialkyl sulfoxide $\mathbf{138k}$ by way of imidation to the corresponding sulfoximines 139a–k using an iron-catalyzed nitrene-transfer reaction and the imidating reagent $N\cdot$ -tosyliminophenyliodinane $({\mathrm{PhI}}{=}{\mathrm{NTs}})$ . A broad range of conditions were tested including reaction time, temperature, amount of $\mathbf{PhI=NTs}$ , solvent, and a swathe of catalyst combinations. For the optimized conditions in terms of time, yield, and ee, yields of the sulfoximines 139a–k ranged from $35\mathrm{-}43\%$ except for $\mathbf{1399}$ and 139j which were only 4 and $6\%$ , respectively, while ee’s ranged from $80\mathrm{-}88\%$ as determined by chiral HPLC except for ${\bf1399}$ and 139k which were only 58 and $62\%$ , respectively. In a particular case, $\mathbf{1399}$ could be obtained with an ee of $94\%$ , though only in a reduced yield of $15\%$ . Optimal conditions for obtaining the unreacted enantiomer were not systematically sought but it could be obtained in seemingly reasonable yields of $47-56\%$ though with only very poor ee’s of $56\mathrm{-}74\%$ accounting for the excess mass. Using the optimal ligand with an $^{R,R}$ configuration preferentially transformed the $R$ enantiomer in each of $\mathbf{138a-k}$ with absolute configurations made by comparison of optical rotations to reported data or by analogy to the other compounds examined.
Finally, the thermodynamically driven resolution of 2-hydroxyphenyl alkyl sulfoxides 103d– $\mathbf{g}$ (Scheme 39) has been reported123 by Ye et al. using a chiral-at-metal iridium(III) complex. The diastereomeric excess of the diastereomeric complex was high at ${>}98\%$ with preferential complexation of the $R$ enantiomers of 103d– $\mathbf{g}$ when utilizing the L enantiomer of the complex by displacement of two acetonitrile ligands from the complex and replacement coordination by the S and phenol O atoms of the sulfoxides to the iridium metal. The $s$ enantiomers of $\bf{103d-g}$ which were left essentially uncomplexed could be isolated in $46\mathrm{-}47\%$ yield with an ee of $97{-}99\%$ . Not only could the $R$ enantiomers be recovered from the complex by simple treatment with trifluoroacetic acid in acetonitrile in $45\mathrm{-}47\%$ yield with an ee of $98\mathrm{-}99\%$ , but the iridium complex itself could also be recovered in high yield $(86-91\%)$ for re-use with no subsequent loss of performance. Altogether a highly effective and efficient process! Absolute configurations of the iridium complex with 103f were determined by X-ray crystallography and then for the other substrates and their complexes with iridium by comparison of their ECD spectra. Ee’s were determined by both chiral HPLC and ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR using a CSA.
## 5. Other methods
Various preparation methods for chiral sulfoxides that were unable to be placed in preceding sections are covered here and include nucleophilic displacement at the sulfur atom for desymmetrization of achiral sulfoxides, enantioselective recognition and separation based on either metal–organic frameworks (MOF’s) or host–guest chemistry, and the Horner–Wadsworth– Emmons reaction. The latter is an efficient means for the preparation of $\lvert\mathfrak{a},\mathfrak{\beta}$ -unsaturated sulfoxides from $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -phosphorylmethyl sulfoxides and aldehydes, important intermediates widely used for carbon–carbon bond formation and as dienophiles in Diels– Alder reactions.
In 2012 Br¨uckner et al. reported124 the first example of the stereoselective synthesis of chiral aryl alkyl sulfoxides by the desymmetrization of diaryl sulfoxides 140 via a sulfoxide– magnesium exchange reaction (Scheme 40). Using di-isopropylmagnesium in the presence of nBuLi and ligand testing three (S)-BINOL ligands in addition to other compounds as the chiral selectors (CS’s) on di- $p$ -tolyl sulfoxide (140c), only (S)-BINOL (142) provided reasonable enantioselectivity for the iso-propyl $p$ -tolyl sulfoxide (141c) product. The $s$ enantiomer [or effectively the racemate when the enantiomeric excess (ee) was ${<}1\%]$ happened to be produced in all cases despite a variety of ligands being tested, though paradoxically the monomethyl ether of (S)-BINOL (142) yielded the $R$ enantiomer in contrast to (S)-BINOL (142) itself. Altogether, ten diaryl sulfoxides under optimized conditions were desymmetrized to their corresponding chiral iso-propyl aryl sulfoxides 141 in moderate-to-excellent yields $(70-91\%$ , with the exception of the sterically hindered 140g, ${<}54\%$ ) and with good-to-excellent enantioselectivities $(70–91\%$ ee). The BINOL ligands could also be recovered almost quantitatively, thus making the method suitable for large-scale applications.
The workers also reported the functionalization of enantioenriched 141b $70\%$ ee), firstly by methylation of the iso-propyl group to a tert-butyl group, and then by phosphorylation of the phenyl group to a 2-diphenylphosphoryl phenyl group. Both transformations rely on the supporting role of the sulfoxide group for reaction to occur. Significantly, the ee was maintained at the same level in the products, thus alluding to further means to produce chiral sulfoxides albeit by using enantioenriched starting material.
Later, Bru¨ckner and Ruppenthal reported125 an expansion of the dialkylmagnesium compound utilized for the desymmetrization of diaryl sulfoxides 140 to chiral sulfoxides. Reactions were conducted under similar conditions as previously124 but using only (S)-BINOL as the CS. Several primary dialkylmagnesium compounds $[\mathrm{{alkyl}=M e}$ , Et, nBu, nHex, $\mathbf{-CH_{2}C H_{2}P h}$ , Bn, iBu, cHex) complexed with the dilithium salt of $(S)$ -BINOL (142), generated in situ as previously using nBuLi, were found to work exceedingly well with diaryl sulfoxides 140 to yield the corresponding alkyl aryl sulfoxides. Thirty-two examples in total were described with yields of up to $\sim100\%$ and ee’s of up to $97\%$ in some cases, though yields, ee’s, and reaction times were all extremely variable depending on the substrate and dialkylmagnesium compound utilized for reasons that are yet to be clarified.125 As previously,124 only the $s$ enantiomer was produced in all cases. The methodology represents enormous potential for the synthesis of chiral aryl alkyl sulfoxides from readily available starting materials.
Tan et al. reported126 the stereoselective synthesis of sulfoxides 144 (Scheme 41) based on the alkylation of sulfenates 143A generated in situ from methyl esters 143 using halogenated chiral pentanidiums 145 as phase-transfer catalysts (PTC’s) and CS’s. Although the reaction was conducted at low temperature $(-70{}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}){}_{:}$ the advantage of this system is that only $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ , or even as little as $0.25\mathrm{\mol\%}$ , of chiral ligand is sufficient for the transformation providing heterocyclic sulfoxides 144 with high enantioselectivities (up to $99\%$ ee) and in high yields (up to $99\%$ ). In addition, the method is amenable for compounds containing groups that are incompatible with strong oxidants or organometallic reagents such as thienyl sulfoxides. The $R$ enantiomer was produced in the case of 3-methylbenzo $[b]$ thiophene benzyl sulfoxide as evidenced by X-ray crystallographic structure determination. Sulfoxide products were purified by column chromatography and ee’s determined by chiral HPLC. Computational studies and mechanistic investigations indicate that the reactions proceed through sulfenates as the key intermediates with the release of methyl acrylate, the process being assisted by multiple non-covalent interactions.
Similar methodology was undertaken by Perrio et al.127 for the enantioselective $s{\mathrm{.}}$ -alkylation of benzyl, $n$ -dodecyl, and cyclohexyl sulfenates with methyl iodide, the sulfenates likewise generated in situ from their corresponding 2-(phenylsulfinyl)- ethyl sulfoxides, $\mathrm{PhSO_{2}C H_{2}C H_{2}S(O)R}$ $\mathbf{\dot{R}_{\alpha}}=\mathbf{\Phi}$ benzyl, $n$ -dodecyl, cyclohexyl), but using a commercially available chiral cinchonidinium as PTC and CS. Under optimized conditions – $33\%$ aqueous NaOH, toluene– $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ $\left(8:2\right)$ , $5\ \mathrm{mol\%}$ catalyst, and methyl iodide (5 equivalents) at 2 $5\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\ -\ \left(R\right)$ -benzyl methyl sulfoxide was isolated in $60\%$ yield with $38\%$ ee while $(R)$ -cyclohexyl methyl sulfoxide was produced in $34\%$ yield with $47\%$ ee and $(R)–n\cdot$ -dodecyl methyl sulfoxide was produced in $43\%$ yield with $20\%$ ee. The replacement of the phenylsulfinyl group with a nitro group as the electron-withdrawing group was also tested, but the reaction of 2-nitroethyl $n$ -dodecyl sulfoxide was disappointing with a poor yield $(20\%)$ and abysmal enantioselectivity $(7\%$ ee). The work was significant for expanding the substrate scope of the methodology to $n$ -alkyl (cycloalkyl, benzylic) alkyl sulfoxides and as well for gaining further insight into the nature of the non-covalent bindings involved in the reaction course since $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking can be ruled out. Ee’s were determined by chiral HPLC while absolute configurations were assigned by comparison of optical rotations and/or HPLC retention times to literature values.
So¨derman and Schwan128 also reported (Scheme 42) on the reaction of lithium sulfenates 153 with chiral $N\cdot$ -Boc-protected $\upbeta$ -substituted $\upbeta$ -amino iodides 149 as a means to obtain by a different synthetic approach, chiral $\upbeta$ -substituted $\upbeta\mathrm{.}$ -amino sulfoxides 150–152. They examined three sets of substrates, arenesulfenates [from methyl (Z)-2- $\mathit{\Pi}_{p}$ -tolylsulfinyl)acrylate (146)], which provided yields from $71{-}92\%$ while diastereomeric ratios (dr’s) were often near 9 : 1 (range, 63 : 37–94 : 6);
alkanesulfenates (from ethyl esters 147), which, though less reactive, could still provide yields of $42\mathrm{-}78\%$ in successful cases and be obtained with dr’s of $78:22-91:9$ ; and $(E)$ -1-alkenesulfenates (from anti-1-alkenyl thiirane $s$ -oxides 148), which demonstrated high reactivity, returning yields of $60\text{\textperthousand}$ and dr’s often close to 9 : 1 (range, $80:20{\-}95:5)$ . Of note, (S)- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\upbeta}$ -amino iodide electrophiles yielded $({R_{\mathrm{S}}},{S_{\mathrm{C}}}){-\upbeta}$ -amino sulfoxides whereas (R)-amino iodides afforded $(S_{\mathrm{{S}}},R_{\mathrm{{C}}})–\upbeta$ -amino sulfoxides (with two naming exceptions for the alkyl sulfoxides 151 due only to a change in atomic priority), thus the stereochemical course of the reaction was determined solely by the configuration of the electrophile and was independent of any substitution in the electrophile. A comprehensive range of conditions were tested in the study, e.g. the metal counter ion (lithium was superior), solvent, temperature, etc., to find optimal conditions for yields and dr’s. A transition state model was also proposed to account for the stereochemical course of the reaction and also for the high reactivity of the electrophile. Dr’s were determined by chiral HPLC and configurations assigned by comparison of optical rotations or $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR, consistency of HPLC retention times, and, where available, by comparison to literature retention times. Configurations were also unequivocally confirmed in selected cases by X-ray crystallographic analysis.
A synthetic route for the preparation of chiral $\mathfrak{a}$ -alkoxy 156 and $\mathfrak{a}$ -amino-alkyl 157 $p$ -tolyl sulfoxides (Scheme 43) based on Andersen’s methodology4 has been reported129 by Horan et al. The reaction started with the asymmetric deprotonation of an $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ -hybridized carbon atom bearing a heteroatom in substrate 154 in the presence of a chiral diamine. Nucleophilic attack of the resulting lithium salt intermediate onto Phillips’ sulfinate130 $[(1R,2S,5R)-(-)-($ O-menthyl $(S)\/{-}p\/$ -toluenesulfinate, 155] then afforded the corresponding chiral $\mathfrak{a}$ -alkoxy 156 or $\mathfrak{a}$ -amino-alkyl 157 $p$ -tolyl sulfoxides with moderate yields and diastereoselectivities. Though the poor stereospecificity in the reaction at sulfur in 155 resulted in syn and anti diastereomers for each of 156 and 157, they could each be separated chromatographically to yield samples with high dr’s, $\geq99:1$ , and indeed provided a means to obtain both enantiomers as extremely high enantioselectivities were possible depending on the diamine and $\geq98\%$ ee’s could be obtained for both the syn and anti diastereomers of 156 and the syn diastereomer of 157. The $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -alkoxy 156 and $\mathfrak{a}$ -amino-alkyl 157 $p$ -tolyl sulfoxides were subsequently used for preparing enantiopure $\mathfrak{a}$ -functionalized
Grignard reagents by sulfoxide–magnesium exchange with isopropylmagnesium chloride. The chiral iso-propyl $p{\cdot}$ -tolyl sulfoxides resulting from the reaction, though labeled as byproducts, were nonetheless afforded.
Duttwyler et al. also reported56 a nucleophilic arylation method for the preparation of optically active phenolic $p$ -tolyl sulfoxides 159 (Scheme 44). The strategy also used Phillips’ sulfinate 155 as the CS and was likewise based on Andersen’s methodology4 for the generation of the chiral sulfoxide. Two slightly altered methods particular for the preparation of each of 69 and ${\bf158a,b}$ were described. In the case of phenylsubstituted phenol 69, it can react directly with nBuLi to give the aryl lithium, followed by nucleophilic substitution of 155 to provide the corresponding phenolic $p$ -tolyl sulfoxide 159a. However, for 1-naphthyl- or $p$ -phenyl phenyl-substituted phenols 158b,c, additional use of NBS was required to afford sulfoxides 159b,c. As the method is not suitable for preparing phenolic tert-butyl sulfoxides, an asymmetric catalyzed oxidation to access the tert-butyl sulfoxides was developed56 (see Section 2. Chemical asymmetric oxidation).
Fern´andez et al., building on their previous results, have described131 an improved use of diacetone-D-glucose (‘‘DAG methodology’’) as the CS for the stereoselective synthesis of $(S_{\mathrm{S}})$ -tert-butyl sulfoxides 162 from racemic tert-butylsulfinyl chloride (160Aa) via diacetone-D-glucose sulfinate ester $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ -161a (Scheme 45). Initial testing of a variety of conditions provided variable results (yields, de’s, and diastereoselectivity) depending the system – substrate sulfinyl chloride 160A, base used, and protecting groups on the glucose unit. Indeed it is well known that different bases can induct alternate diastereoselectivity in the DAG methodology as well varying results. This is because the base has an instrumental role in determining the diastereoselectivity due both to its direct participation in the displacement of chloride by the glucose unit and its ability to racemize sulfinyl chloride 160A, thus effecting a dynamic kinetic resolution of the sulfinyl chloride 160A. With optimal base selection – triethylamine in the case of tert-butylsulfinyl chloride (160Aa) and diacetone-D-glucose; H¨unig’s base (N,N-di-iso-propylethylamine, DIPEA) in the case of methyl and iso-propylsulfinyl chlorides (160Ab,c) with diacetone-D-glucose and dicyclohexylidene-Dglucose, respectively – modest-to-excellent yields $(74-100\%)$ and diastereoselectivities (74 to $>96\%$ de’s as determined by ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR on the crude mixtures) for the diacetone-D-glucose sulfinate esters $(S_{\mathrm{{S}}})$ -161 were obtained. Crucially, the addition of a sub-stoichiometric of 4-(dimethylamino)pyridine (DMAP) to the reactions resulted in the other diastereomer being produced in each case, viz. diacetone-D-glucose sulfinate esters $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ -161. Thus, catalysis by DMAP determines the stereochemical outcome of the reaction. Additionally, excellent yields $(96-98\%)$ and modest-to-excellent diastereoselectivities $62{-}94\%$ de’s) were also obtained using DMAP and, in the case of 160Aa at least, its inclusion improved the stereoselectivity. Furthermore, it also enhanced the rate of the reaction in all instances.
The diacetone glucose moiety in diacetone-D-glucose sulfinate ester $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)^{}$ -161a was then displaced using organometallic reagents (Scheme 46) to provide the tert-butyl alkyl sulfoxides 162 in moderate-to-good yields. Inversion of configuration occurred upon reaction of $\mathbf{\left({{R}_{S}}\right)\mathbf{-161}}$ with the organometallics to yield $(S_{\mathrm{S}})$ -configured products $\mathbf{(}S_{\mathrm{S}})–\mathbf{162}$ (except when atomic priority naming deems otherwise) with no loss in enantiopurity as confirmed by chiral HPLC. The absolute configuration of $\mathbf{(}S_{\mathrm{S}})–162\mathbf{a}$ was confirmed by both optical rotation and circular dichroism in comparison to literature values. The methodology represents an improvement for the synthesis of enantiopure tert-butyl sulfoxides 162 from readily available and inexpensive D-glucose. The diacetone-D-glucose sulfinate ester $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ -161a was also treated with LiHMDS for the preparation of enantiopure $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ -tert-butyl sulfinamides (following a second-step treatment with methanol and silica) and $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ -tert-butyl sulfinylimines (following a second-step addition to a suspension of CsF and an aldehyde). Enantiopure $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ -tert-butyl sulfinamides could also be prepared from $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ -161a by treatment with freshly prepared lithium alkyl amides.
Using the Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction, Sun et al. have reported132 a stereoselective synthesis of chiral unsaturated tert-butyl sulfoxides (Scheme 47). Firstly, they synthesized diethyl tert-butylsulfinylmethylphosphonate (164) in $89\%$ ee through nucleophilic substitution of the tert-butylsulfanyl group in (S)-S-tert-butyl 2-methylpropane-2-thiosulfinate (163) by the anion of diethyl methylphosphonate generated in situ using nBuLi. The Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction was then conducted on sulfoxide 164 with a variety of aldehydes using nBuLi. The reactions showed good-to-excellent stereoselectivities for the $E$ configuration resulting in $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated sulfoxides 165 with moderate-to-good yields $(61-90\%)$ and excellent enantioselectivities $87-91\%$ ee).
Soon after, Mikołajczyk et al. developed133 a stereoselective synthesis of chiral $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated sulfoxides with stereoselectivity for the $Z$ configuration using a similar Horner–Wadsworth– Emmons reaction. With (S)-(diphenoxyphosphoryl)methyl $p{\cdot}$ tolyl sulfoxide as starting material, the reaction with aldehydes proceeded well providing yields of $85\mathrm{-}94\%$ . Although an interesting $Z$ -selective reaction, the stereoselectivity was highly variable, from modest $\left(3:1\right)$ to excellent (97 : 3), and even inversed $\left(1:2\right)$ . From the optimization-of-the-conditions process and substratescope studies, it was assessed that the stereoselectivity depends on several reaction parameters such as base, aldehyde, temperature, and reaction additives. As well, the substrate scope may be very limited with only five examples presented and ee’s were not measured and the products were only assumed to be enantiopure based on the enantiopurity of the starting material.
Recently, Cui et al. described134 MOF’s that can effect the enantioseparation of alkyl aryl sulfoxides 2 (Scheme 48). Two chiral 2,3-dihydroimidazo[1,2-a]pyridine (DHIP, 167)-based MOF’s 168 were prepared from amino alcohol 166 with the Zn-DHIP MOF demonstrating enantioseparation ability for racemic alkyl aryl sulfoxides 2 while the Cd-DHIP MOF displayed virtually no enantioseparation ability. Nine examples of racemic alkyl aryl sulfoxides 2 were investigated with resulting ee’s in the range $12\mathrm{-}47\%$ as determined by chiral HPLC with the best ee being obtained for methyl phenyl sulfoxide (2b). Interestingly, depending on seemingly inconsequential changes in the aryl moiety, either the $R$ or the $s$ enantiomer could be preferentially included. Notably, the MOF’s were easily recovered and able to be re-used without a significant reduction in their enantioseparation ability. Although this method only provided poor-tomodest enantioseparation, narrow substrate scope, and was unpredictable, it nonetheless provides an alternative means for enantioseparation and may enhance practical applications of MOF chemistry.
A chiral MOF has also been reported135 by Liu et al. in tandem with nanoliquid chromatography for the enantioseparation of 2b. A monolithic poly(4-vinylpyridine-co-ethylene dimethacrylate) column without the inclusion of a chiral MOF exhibited no enantioseparation ability while two baselineseparated peaks using hexane as the eluent were observed by the combination of a polymer monolithic column and a porous chiral $\mathbf{Zn-MOF}$ , $[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}|$ (benzene dicarboxylate)(L-lactic acid)(DMF)]. This study may also extend the practical application of MOF’s as well as being useful for chiral liquid chromatography.
Finally, Hirose et al. have developed136 a methodology based on host–guest chemistry for the enantioseparation of racemic alkyl aryl sulfoxides 2 (Scheme 49). Chiral supramolecular hosts 169 comprising urea-modified L-phenylalanines and various achiral regular primary amines for enantioselection were prepared. The supramolecular host enantioselectively interacts with the racemic alkyl aryl sulfoxides 2 resulting in enantioseparation owing to the formation of ternary inclusion crystals. Optimization of the amines and ureas revealed that diphenylmethylamine and $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -(phenylcarbamoyl)-L-phenylalanine was the best combination for the enantioseparation of 2. Overall, for the eight examples of racemic alkyl aryl sulfoxides 2 successfully resolved, enantioselectivities ranged from modest-to-excellent $(52\mathrm{-}89\%$ ee) as determined by chiral HPLC with the best ee again being obtained for 2b. The stereochemical preference of the process depended mainly on the structure of the sulfoxides, e.g. three of the four methyl aryl sulfoxides were enriched in the $R$ enantiomer while the sulfoxides with bulkier alkyl groups, together with the one methyl exception, were conversely all enriched in the $s$ enantiomer. The proposed basis for the reversed enantioselectivity for the bulkier sulfoxides is the different packing arrangements of the 1D hydrogen-bonding networks.
6. The SDE phenomenon and the problem of accurate determination of the stereochemical outcome
For the isolation and analysis of sulfoxides such as methyl tolyl sulfoxide (2a) resulting from catalytic asymmetric sulfoxidation as pioneered independently by Kagan13 and Modena,14 standard work-up included flash chromatography over silica gel using EtOAc to remove the remaining unreacted sulfide 1a, the byproduct sulfone resulting from over-oxidation, and the catalyst. Prior to the late 1980’s, HPLC using chiral stationary phases was not readily available137 and the enantiomeric excess’s (ee’s) of the product 2a were evaluated either by $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR using a chiral shift reagent or by measurement of the optical rotation.138 In 1994, when Kagan’s group began10 using chiral HPLC for determining the stereochemical outcome, the results were rather surprising. It seemed that this significantly more accurate analytical method revealed ‘‘poor reproducibility in the ee’s’’10 of the analyzed product 2a. Eventually the authors10 discovered that the ee’s of the product 2a were strongly dependent on the chromatographic isolation process. In particular, rather noticeable enantioenrichment occurred for the early fractions while enantiodepletion was observed for the latter fractions. For example, as presented in Scheme 50, when a sample of $\mathbf{\Pi}(R){-}2\mathbf{a}$ $(0.82g,86.0\%$ ee) was subjected to routine flash chromatography
Scheme 50 Achiral-phase flash chromatography of sulfoxide (R)-2a of $86\%$ ee. The collection of fractions containing (R)-2a ranged in ee from $73-99.5\%$ .
using neat EtOAc as eluent and fourteen $20~\mathrm{mL}$ fractions were collected and analyzed, it was found that the ee’s of these fractions were not constant and instead varied considerably.
Thus, while the first fraction contained almost enantiopure (R)-2a $99.5\%$ ee), for successive fractions, the ee’s gradually decreased with the final fraction containing significantly enantiodepleted (R)-2a $73.0\%$ ee). It needs to be strongly emphasized that additional optical activity was not created by this process – the recombination of all fourteen fractions would generate a sample of the same weight and enantiopurity as that of the original sample. Thus, the initial $86.0\%$ ee of the sample simply disproportionated to $99.5\%$ ee in the first fraction and to $73.0\%$ ee in the last fraction while the material taken as a whole retains the same $86.0\%$ ee as before the chromatography.
This discovery was a remarkable revelation with regards to the stereochemical course of the reaction, implying that the catalytic asymmetric oxidation of sulfide 1a could be mistakenly reported as having an extremely high enantioselectivity $(99.5\%$ ee) or only a moderate enantioselectivity $(73.0\%$ ee), depending on which particular fraction the practitioner chose to analyze. Furthermore, the phenomenon was found to be very general, being observed for structurally different sulfoxides using various achiral stationary phases and various eluents (vide infra).10 The phenomenon has now been given the general descriptor selfdisproportionation of enantiomers (SDE),9 and in this case, SDE via chromatography given the applied process. As pointed out by Kagan et al.,10 ‘‘Evaluation of the enantioselectivity of an asymmetric synthesis of sulfoxides requires great care in the workup and isolation to avoid fractionation of enantiomers’’.
The reader is referred to these articles for insight into the SDE phenomenon.
The term SDE does not infer anything regarding the mechanistic aspects of the process, but rather refers only to the final outcome, i.e., the concurrent formation of enantioenriched and -depleted fractions under totally achiral conditions external to the sample itself. In addition to chromatography, other processes exhibiting manifestation of the SDE phenomenon include force field and physicochemical phase transitions. In the case of SDE via force field, the density differences between racemic and enantiopure crystals154 can yield differential translational movement in a liquid (density flotation155) or acceleration by density gradient ultracentrifugation156 facilitating the collection of samples of enantioenriched and -depleted composition. The differences in the crystallographic structures of racemic and enantiopure samples also give rise to the SDE via phase transitions. For example, differences in solubility and rates of deposition lead to SDE via crystallization, i.e. fractional crystallization,157 a well known process. Not dissimilarly, SDE via sublimation152 also stems from the differences in the energies of the crystallographic lattices of racemic and enantiopure crystals and the consequent vapor pressures. This process is seemingly much less known but was shown to have a preparative advantage over the commonly used fractional crystallization.158 Another case of SDE via phase transitions is distillation,159 though such examples are exceedingly rare and the process is limited to compounds possessing quite extraordinary intermolecular hydrogen bonding.
Due to the overwhelming pervasiveness of some form of chromatography being applied during the work-up and purification of the product from a reaction, SDE via achiral chromatography is by far the most general and common manifestation of the SDE phenomenon,150 even if workers are oblivious to the very existence and the consequent effects of the phenomenon. Similar to the distinct differences between the crystallographic structures of racemic and enantiopure crystals, chiral compounds, either in a liquid state or in a solution, show a preference for either homo- or heterochiral molecular associations. The formation of homo- or heterochiral higher-order species in solution has been well used to rationalize various non-linear physicochemical properties of scalemic samples such as optical rotation and chiral catalyst activation/poisoning.160 The single molecules and the homo- and heterochiral molecular associations all have different chromatographic behavior and this is the basis for the SDE via achiral chromatography phenomenon. Taking into account that intermolecular interaction is an intrinsic occurrence for all compounds, SDE via achiral chromatography should always be anticipated whenever a scalemic sample is subjected to chromatographic purification. As a consequence there are numerous reports of SDE via achiral chromatography occurring for compounds exhibiting intermolecular interactions involving hydrogen bonding,148,150,151 dipole–dipole,10,141,161,162 or $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking.139,141,163 The reports cover virtually all types of liquid chromatography, gravity-driven column chromatography, flash, HPLC, MPLC, SEC, etc. Stationary phases where the phenomenon has been observed include normal polar phases (silica gel, alumina, etc.) as well as reversed-phases.148a,149,150a,c,153
From numerous research data149–151 and the structure of a compound, it is possible to make quite reasonable predictions regarding the expected magnitude of the SDE. Thus while any chiral compound can, given the right conditions, exhibit SDE via chromatography, some functional groups tend to impart a propensity for the phenomenon and hence the concept of SDE-phoric groups was recently introduced.164 As depicted in Fig. 48, amides of chiral amines164–166 (170), $\mathfrak{a}$ -amino acid esters167 (171), and $\upbeta$ -amino acid esters168 (172) together with trifluoromethyl-containing compounds147,151,152a,169,170 (173), and of course, sulfoxides10,161,162,171 (2), are all classes of compounds that readily seem to exhibit SDE via chromatography, and moreover, do so strongly, thus earning themselves the moniker SDE-phoric groups.
Nonetheless, the pertinent question therefore arises, how to address the problem of the SDE phenomenon in terms of its effect on the sample and the measurement of the ee, particularly for SDE via chromatography, by workers in general? What steps should they take to avoid the SDE or to prove that alteration to the SDE has not occurred during the isolation or purification of a compound from a reaction? For purification processes, this may require that the SDE phenomenon does not occur under the applied conditions, and/or performing before and after measurements of the ee (e.g., by the use of chiral chromatography or chiral NMR). For isolation processes, any alterations to the ee due to the SDE via chromatography can, to a degree, be avoided if it is reasonable to collect all of the fractions containing the compound of interest. However, even if it is the case that the entire eluting peak can be collected, one may choose not to do so to simply limit large collections of solvent or to be overly cautious by limiting the possibility that the collection contains other components buried in the tail or the front of the peak. Collection of the entire peak of interest may mean in some instances that it has to be collected together with unwanted components and then chromatography re-applied under new conditions to attain the isolate in required purity – an unavoidable price to maintain the integrity of the original sample ee. However, the ‘‘collection of all material’’ in chromatography can only minimize, but not completely eliminate, mistakes in the determination of ee and moreover, one has to be cognizant of the fact that due to the tailing phenomenon in chromatography, near $100\%$ collection of a sample is not feasible or practical. Even though the tails of a peak in chromatography may contain only minimal material, by the SDE phenomenon they can potentially contain very close to racemic or very enantiopure material, thus potentially perturbing the ee of the collected material markedly.
For chromatography, while it is quite usual in the case of HPLC to report some of the conditions precisely, e.g. flow rate, eluent composition, and so forth, more could be reported, for example, the number of fractions collected and the volume of each. This consideration also needs to be extended to traditional column chromatography whereby the flow rates, fraction volumes, etc. should be reported in sufficient detail to ensure reproducibility.161,164,165,166b,168 It is worth emphasizing that the ee determination of samples by chiral analysis before and after any physicochemical process applied to a scalemic sample is strongly recommended. Certainly only the chiral analysis conducted on the crude product or crude isolate should be used to make inferences or deductions regarding the stereoselective outcome of a reaction and it should be made clear when and at what stage the chiral analysis was performed. At all times workers should take pains to prove that the ee’s of the samples are not being altered and that they have taken appropriate steps to eliminate as much as possible any sources of change in the ee’s of the samples, e.g. by collecting as much of the peak as possible in chromatography, not subjecting volatile samples to high vacuum or high temperatures for extended periods of time during the removal of solvent steps, etc.
In Kagan’s seminal paper,10 other sulfoxides examined in addition to methyl $p$ -tolyl, (2a) and found to provide a strong magnitude of the SDE via achiral chromatography phenomenon included benzyl tert-butyl (174), ferrocenyl phenyl (175), and ferrocenyl methyl (176) sulfoxide (Fig. 49). Using enantioenriched samples of methyl $p$ -tolyl sulfoxide (2a), SDE via chromatography was clearly observable on a number of stationary phases – silica gel, alumina, and reverse-phase silica gel – with both silica gel and reverse-phase silica gel proving superior to alumina using ethyl acetate as the eluting solvent in each instance. In each case, the first fraction was more enriched in the excess enantiomer (both $R^{-}$ and $s.$ -enantioenriched samples were tested) compared to the ee of the starting sample; for silica gel and reverse-phase silica gel, fractions of $99.5\%$ ee could be obtained from starting ee’s of 86.0 and $91.0\%$ , respectively, while alumina provided a first fraction of $91.0\%$ ee from a starting ee of $86\%$ . Since this was an early work in the study of the SDE via achiral chromatography phenomenon, the workers took pains to prove that the changes were not an artifact of the chromatographic process, e.g. by testing enantiopure material whereby the ee was found to remain constant across the elution profile. Adjusting the ratio of stationary phase (silica gel) to substrate either up or down did not provide improved results, in fact both attempts depreciated the SDE magnitude, particularly the latter. Similar results under the optimized conditions with the early fractions enriched in the excess enantiomer were obtained for benzyl tert-butyl (174) and ferrocenyl methyl (176) sulfoxide [except that ethyl acetate–diethyl ether (1 : 1 ratio) was used as the eluting solvent for 176] and for these three compounds, 2a, 176, and 174, the SDE magnitudes ranged from $9.5\substack{-36.5\%}$ ee. Except in the case of ferrocenyl phenyl (175), the change in ee was always in the same direction as evidenced by plots of the ee of each collected fraction vs. each collected fraction.
Zhou et al. examined162 scalemic samples of prazoles (Fig. 50), viz. the drugs $(S)$ -esomeprazole (9a), $(R)$ -lansoprazole (9b), $(R)$ -rabeprazole (9c), and (S)-pantoprazole (9d). These pharmacologically important and commercially valuable proton pump inhibitors, which are commonly used for the treatment of peptic ulcers, were all originally supplied as racemic samples. Currently, esomeprazole (9a) (the moniker esomeprazole refers specifically the $s$ enantiomer while when denoting the compound as omeprazole, the absolute configuration is undeclared and may refer to either enantiomer or the racemate) is marketed as a single enantiomer (the $s$ configured enantiomer) while the $R$ enantiomer of lansoprazole $\mathbf{(96)}$ has also been approved for commercial use. Thus there is considerable interest in not only obtaining these compounds enantiopure, but ensuring that correct evaluation of the stereochemical outcome of a reaction is reliable and accurate and that industrial processes are not unwittingly compromised by misunderstood phenomena. Using an enantioenriched sample of (S)-esomeprazole (9a, $76.2\%$ ee) as a model substrate, optimization of the conditions using silica gel as stationary phase in regular column chromatography with slight application of pressure was performed testing variables such as solvent (seven in all were tried), ratio of substrate to stationary phase, stationary phase mesh size, etc. Although ethyl acetate– $\mathrm{\cdotCH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ ( $\overset{\cdot}{2}:1$ ratio) provided the best SDE magnitude ( $38.6\%$ ee, highest single fraction ee of $99.8\%$ ), sample decomposition meant that methyl tert-butyl ether was the preferred solvent providing an SDE magnitude of $30.6\%$ ee and highest single fraction ee of $93.4\%$ . Testing all four prazoles 9 under the optimal conditions with low $(17-22\%$ ee), intermediate $(59-64\%$ ee), and high $(82-89\%$ ee) starting ee’s consistently provided SDE magnitudes in the range $14{-}30\%$ ee (except for 9b which provided an SDE magnitude of $6\%$ ee for the low starting ee value). Moreover, for each of the four prazoles 9, chromatographic runs with the high starting ee value provided effectively enantiopure ( ${\cdot}>99.9\%$ ee) samples for the first eluting fraction. Plots of the ee of each collected fraction vs. each collected fraction did not reveal any anomalies and the change in ee was always in the same direction with ee’s being measured accurately using chiral HPLC.
The authors accounted for the SDE behavior by formulating a dimeric association between the molecules (Fig. 51) based on the heterochiral dimer 177 described171 by Mazzotti et al. for omeprazole (9a) who determined experimentally that the heterochiral dimer was more stable $(K=25.2\pm4.0\mathrm{\M}^{-1})$ than the homochiral dimer $(K=14.0\pm3.5\ \mathrm{M}^{-1})$ by optical rotation measurements in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ . The hydrogen bonding-based association seems reasonable given that using a solvent mixture of $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ –methanol (25 : 1 ratio), the SDE practically disappears (SDE magnitude, $2.0\%$ ee) in comparison to the authors’ results with tert-butyl ether.
The in-depth and detailed study161 by Soloshonok et al. of the SDE via achiral chromatography phenomenon using methyl $n$ -pentyl sulfoxide (178, Fig. 52) as the model substrate and regular gravity-driven column chromatography revealed much about the phenomenon. The workers methodically examined various solvent systems (eight altogether), water content of the eluting solvent, mesh size of the stationary phase, flow rates, and the ratio of the substrate to the stationary phase while the accuracy of their results was incontrovertibly determined by chiral GC.
Using ethyl acetate–cyclohexane (5 : 1 ratio) as eluent and silica gel as stationary phase, it was easy to obtain enantiopure $(>99.9\%$ ee) material in the first fraction starting from an ee of only $34.6\%$ , thus dispelling emphatically the notion that to obtain enantiopure material by SDE via achiral chromatography it was necessary to start with a relatively high ee. The usefulness of this important result is readily apparent, but so are the ramifications regarding the accurate determination of the stereochemical outcome of an asymmetric reaction if due care is not taken. Indeed, the SDE was so strong – the highest magnitude of the SDE recorded was $93.5\%$ ee – that it was almost not possible to suppress it, even with the inclusion of acetone or methanol as part of the eluent. Nevertheless, the SDE yield for enantiopure material was considerably higher if the ee of the starting material was higher, e.g., ca. four-fold higher for a starting ee of $55.2\%$ ee cf. $34.6\%$ ee.
One aspect of the SDE phenomenon, particularly for SDE via chromatography, that is concerning is that workers will sometimes report a series of high ee values for one substrate, or a subset of substrates, then inexplicably report a much lower ee value for another very similar substrate, or a subset of substrates, with seemingly inconsequential structural differences between the compared entities. Is it because there are subtle steric, inductive, or stereoelectronic effects present? Or is it because of the differing chromatographic behaviors of the compounds and thus the peaks were fractionated differently (e.g., due to their proximity to the starting material, by-products, or reagents as a result of differing retention times) prior to determination of the ee? However, for all the remarkable developments that are described in this review, the question of the accurate determination of the stereochemical outcome possibly being compromised by the SDE phenomenon is clearly a problem of considerable concern. What is surprising is that none of the papers concerning the asymmetric synthesis of sulfoxides covered in this review have given assurances about potential inaccuracies in the determination of the ee due to the SDE phenomenon. And clearly sulfoxides are a SDE-phoric group which display a strong magnitude of SDE via achiral chromatography. Moreover, and while there are some exceptions, generally, and very disconcertingly, it is often not clear, at what stage and in what state the ee was measured – on the isolate or on the crude reaction mixture, before or after purification, what processes had been performed on the sample prior to measurement of the ee etc. This is a disheartening state of affairs and we can only strongly urge workers to be meticulous in their reporting of how the work was done with regards to any physicochemical process that is performed on scalemic samples.
Our recommendation is obviously that due care is taken when reporting the stereoselective outcome of a reaction, preferably with the determination of the ee performed on the crude reaction material with little or no physicochemical processing if possible. There is even one report172 in the literature where rotary evaporation of the product led to a change in the ee! Presumably the sample underwent SDE via sublimation during the rotary evaporation to account for the observed changes in ee. Thus the exact procedure taken for, and the circumstances under which, the ee was measured should be stated explicitly in the experimental and/or duly noted in the discussion. Such stringent processing requirements effectively mean that many techniques such as optical rotation and ECD which require the sample to be pure to enable measurement of the ee are not readily applicable. Whenever processing is done, e.g. chromatography of any sort, then the whole of the peak needs to be collected and not fractionated otherwise disproportionation may lead to an erroneous result. Moreover, the disproportionation across a peak depends on the ee of the sample loaded onto the column under a given set of conditions, and the breadth of the peak will likewise also vary depending on the ee.147,161,165,166a,168 Thus, attempts to fractionate peaks in the same manner are not a reliable course of action. While it is clearly apparent that the SDE phenomenon has great, mostly untapped, potential usefulness for the enantiopurification of scalemic samples, there is general ignorance of the phenomenon by the wider research community, in particular, for SDE via achiral chromatography and one might concur that critical discussion of anomalies and errors in the determination of the stereochemical outcome of catalytic enantioselective reactions in general, and those related to sulfoxides in particular, is long overdue. Indeed, many reported results concerning the asymmetric synthesis of sulfoxides contain confusing or questionable data which cannot be rationalized by the usual steric or electronic factors. It is hoped that initiating a critical discussion of the problem of the accurate determination of the stereochemical outcome of a reaction due to the SDE phenomenon will transpire to the broader research community as the SDE phenomenon can readily occur for scalemic samples when subjected to practically any physicochemical process. Furthermore, it is also hoped that discussion of recent results in particular will be initiated and that this will be engaging and motivating for current research-active authors to respond to in their future publications and comment as necessary. Due to the pervasiveness of the phenomenon and its ramifications with respect to the unintentional alteration of the ee of a sample, we can only echo the words of Kagan10 and strongly urge workers to take due care when applying any physicochemical process to scalemic samples of chiral compounds – and sulfoxides especially – whether it be chromatography of any description or any other process since compounds that exhibit strong SDE under one process are likely to also express strong SDE under a number of processes.
## 7. Conclusions
The latest developments in the preparation of optically active sulfoxides have been reviewed covering approaches such as catalytic asymmetric sulfoxidation using either chemical, enzymatic, or hybrid biocatalytic means and kinetic resolution in addition to various other methods. There is a bewildering array of methodologies available to prepare optically active sulfoxides, and there seems no end in sight to the development of means to accomplish such and one can expect the growth to continue unabated. Some of the most spectacular accomplishments have come from enzymatic approaches due to the phenomenal advancements in molecular biology as well as the extraordinary development of hybrid biocatalysts, the limitations of capacity due to substrate toxicity towards the metabolizing organisms and/or enzyme inhibition notwithstanding. Nevertheless, the impressive developments in catalysts for asymmetric oxidation should not be overshadowed with evermore esoteric ligands being developed and applied. However, it is also clear that there is not a universal solution to the problem of producing optically active sulfoxides and as impressive as some of the results presented in this review are, they cannot necessarily be successfully applied, even with very minor modifications such as the addition of a methyl group in an otherwise unassuming position, to other substrates. Moreover, much time and effort can be required to develop an optimized methodology, which is then also not necessarily transferable to other substrates or amenable to other systems. These vagaries are particularly true for the enzymatic approaches but also hold true for all of the other approaches. The high demands with respect to the required investment to optimize a specific reaction are of little concern for industrial applications, but, aside from providing academic challenges, are problematic for general synthetic applications. In consideration of prediction of the outcome of a reaction, whether it be with respect to substrate conversion and selection, chemoselectivity, enantioselectivity, or even stereochemical preference regarding the absolute configuration, predictability is still a difficult proposition generally that is mostly unfulfilled and thus remains the great challenge of the future.
However, for all these remarkable developments, the problem of the accurate determination of the stereochemical outcome due to the self-disproportionation of enantiomers (SDE) phenomenon is clearly a glaring problem in the current state of affairs as not a single paper reviewed in this article provides an indication that the authors are even aware about potential inaccuracies in the stereochemical outcome arising from the SDE phenomenon. Disconcertingly, it is often not clear, apart from some admirable exceptions, at what stage and in what state workers have measured the enantiomeric excess (ee) – on the isolate or on the crude reaction mixture, before or after purification, what processes had been performed on the sample prior to measurement of the ee, etc. Even such a seemingly benign process as rotary evaporation, with presumably the sample undergoing SDE via sublimation, has been shown to affect the ee!172 While this situation is quite disheartening, it was decided not to criticise individual papers reporting questionable data. Rather, it is hoped that authors will find time to take in this commentary and make appropriate adjustments to their procedures and reporting of results in the future. The SDE phenomenon is an overwhelmingly important aspect of asymmetric synthesis that needs to be appreciated and addressed by workers, not just to avoid the problems of reporting or interpreting erroneous results in terms of the stereochemical outcome, whether it be overestimating or underestimating the ee, but also to take advantage of the potential benefit – as an expansion of the repertoire of methods to obtain enantiopure product complementary to other techniques (viz. SDE via chromatography). Relying on erroneous results can obviously lead to discarding or not reporting results due to ‘‘poor ee’’ when in fact the ee might be much better than realized due to enantiodepletion arising from SDE. Certainly reliable results are needed to make valid comparisons between experiments or to gain a better appreciation of the process at hand and hence increase the viability of any formulated prediction hypothesis. Indeed, many reported results concerning the asymmetric synthesis of sulfoxides contain confusing or questionable data which cannot be rationalized by the usual steric or electronic factors. Finally, it is hoped that workers will duly take into consideration the effect that the SDE might have on the accuracy of the stereochemical outcome of a reaction and take appropriate steps to safeguard against unintended perturbations in the workup procedure.
